/* Qadence content · interview question bank · technical topics:
   scenarios (iq-scn), API (iq-api), SQL (iq-sql), mobile (iq-mob), non-functional (iq-nfr). */
QA_CONTENT.addInterview([

  /* ===================== Scenario: “How would you test…?” ===================== */
  {
    id: 'iq-scn-01', cat: 'scenario', level: 'beginner', freq: 'very-common',
    q: 'How would you test a login page?',
    short: 'First clarify the rules (username or email, lockout, MFA, SSO, “remember me”). Then cover valid and invalid logins, field validation, security, session handling, UI and accessibility, browsers and performance — and automate the core paths.',
    answer: '<b>Clarify:</b> username or email? Password rules? Lockout after how many failures? MFA, SSO, “remember me”, forgot-password link?<br><br><b>Functional:</b> valid login lands on the right page for each role; wrong password, unknown user, empty fields; email case-insensitive but password case-sensitive; leading/trailing spaces; Enter key submits.<br><b>Boundary/negative:</b> minimum and maximum lengths, very long input, special characters and Unicode.<br><b>Security:</b> HTTPS only; password masked; the same generic error for a wrong user and a wrong password (no user enumeration); lockout or rate limit after repeated failures; SQL-injection strings handled safely; session cookie flags (HttpOnly, Secure); after logout the Back button doesn’t restore the session; session timeout.<br><b>UI and accessibility:</b> labels, tab order, errors announced by screen readers, show-password toggle.<br><b>Compatibility and performance:</b> main browsers and mobile; response time with many users logging in at once.<br><br><b>Prioritise</b> valid login, invalid login, lockout and session handling. <b>Automate</b> those as smoke tests at API and UI level; keep exploratory checks manual.',
    followups: ['How would you test “remember me”?', 'Login works on Chrome but not on Safari — what would you check?', 'Which of these tests would you automate first, and why?'],
    mistakes: ['Jumping straight into a list of test cases without asking a single clarifying question.', 'Testing only valid and invalid passwords and forgetting security, sessions and accessibility.'],
    lesson: 'wm-forms-ui'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-scn-02', cat: 'scenario', level: 'beginner', freq: 'very-common',
    q: 'How would you test a pen?',
    short: 'Ask what kind of pen it is and who it is for, then test against those needs: writing, ink, mechanism, durability, environment, usability, safety and packaging — showing a structured approach rather than a random list.',
    answer: 'The interviewer wants to see whether you ask questions before testing.<br><br><b>Clarify:</b> ball-point, gel or fountain pen? Who uses it — school children, office staff, pilots? Price point? Is there a spec (ink colour, line width, writing length in metres)?<br><br><b>Functional:</b> writes from the first stroke, consistent line, correct colour, click or cap mechanism works, ink lasts the promised length.<br><b>Negative/edge:</b> writing upside down, on glossy or damp paper, after being left uncapped for a day.<br><b>Durability:</b> dropped from desk height, clicked thousands of times, heavy pressure on the tip.<br><b>Environment:</b> heat, cold, air travel (does it leak at altitude?).<br><b>Usability:</b> grip comfort during long writing, smudging for left-handed users, weight and balance.<br><b>Safety and compliance:</b> non-toxic ink, a cap with an air hole if children use it.<br><b>Packaging and labelling.</b><br><br>Finish by prioritising: for a cheap office pen, smooth writing and no leaks matter most. Base pass/fail on the spec, not personal taste.',
    followups: ['How would you test it if you had only five minutes?', 'What is the single most important test, and why?', 'How would your approach change for a pencil or a stapler?'],
    mistakes: ['Listing random actions such as “I would write with it” without asking what the pen is supposed to do.', 'Forgetting non-functional areas such as safety, durability and usability.'],
    lesson: 'mt-scenarios-cases'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-scn-03', cat: 'scenario', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'common',
    q: 'How would you test an elevator?',
    short: 'Clarify floors, capacity, modes and safety rules, then treat it as a state machine: test calls and stops, doors, load limits, emergency and safety features, power failure, waiting-time performance and accessibility — safety first.',
    answer: '<b>Clarify:</b> how many floors and lifts, maximum load, special modes (service, fire), accessibility needs, applicable safety standards.<br><br>Model it as a <b>state machine</b> — idle, moving up, moving down, doors opening, open, closing, out of service — and test the transitions.<br><br><b>Functional:</b> call buttons on every floor and floor buttons inside; it stops at requested floors in a sensible order; display and arrival chime are correct.<br><b>Boundary:</b> lowest and top floor; load exactly at the limit and just above (overload alarm, refuses to move).<br><b>Safety (highest priority):</b> the door sensor reopens when something blocks it; doors never open between floors; emergency stop, alarm and intercom work; fire mode returns to the ground floor; on power failure it stops safely with emergency lighting.<br><b>Performance:</b> average waiting time at rush hour with many simultaneous calls; several lifts share the load.<br><b>Accessibility:</b> braille, audio announcements, button height, door-open time long enough for wheelchair users.<br><br>Prioritise safety, then core movement, then efficiency. Much of this would be tested on a simulator before real hardware.',
    followups: ['Which states and transitions would you draw in a state diagram?', 'How would you test the scheduling logic when many people call at once?'],
    mistakes: ['Testing only that buttons light up and missing safety features such as door sensors and overload.', 'Not asking about floors, capacity or special modes before designing tests.'],
    lesson: 'mt-state-transition'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-scn-04', cat: 'scenario', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'common',
    q: 'How would you test an ATM cash withdrawal?',
    short: 'Clarify cards, limits and note denominations, then cover the happy path, PIN rules, balance and limit boundaries, failures in the middle of a transaction, security, receipts and accessibility — with the account balance as the key check.',
    answer: '<b>Clarify:</b> which cards (own bank, other banks), daily and per-transaction limits, available notes (e.g. only multiples of 500), languages, fees.<br><br><b>Functional:</b> card → PIN → amount → cash, card and receipt returned; the balance drops by exactly the amount plus any fee — verify in the account record, not only on screen.<br><b>PIN:</b> one or two wrong attempts, card blocked after the allowed number of failures, PIN masked.<br><b>Boundary and equivalence:</b> amount equal to the balance and one unit above; daily limit exactly and above; amounts not payable with available notes; zero; very large values.<br><b>Failures (highest risk):</b> account debited but cash not dispensed; network drop after the debit; power cut mid-transaction; cash cassette empty; receipt paper out; card or cash not collected within the timeout.<br><b>Security:</b> session timeout, no full card number on the receipt, PIN pad shielding.<br><b>Usability and accessibility:</b> languages, audio guidance, readable screen.<br><br>Prioritise money correctness and recovery from failures — a customer debited without receiving cash is the worst outcome.',
    followups: ['How would you test the automatic reversal when cash isn’t dispensed?', 'Which of these tests could run on a simulator instead of a real ATM?'],
    mistakes: ['Testing only the happy path and forgetting failures in the middle of a transaction.', 'Trusting the balance shown on screen instead of checking the actual account record.'],
    lesson: 'mt-boundary'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-scn-05', cat: 'scenario', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'common',
    q: 'How would you test a vending machine?',
    short: 'Clarify payment types, products and rules, then test combinations of money, stock and change, refunds, invalid coins or notes, power failure mid-sale and the operator side — using a decision table so no combination is missed.',
    answer: '<b>Clarify:</b> coins, notes, card or QR payment? Does it give change? Prices, sold-out behaviour, refund button?<br><br><b>Functional:</b> exact money → product dispensed; more money → correct change; less money → asks for more and dispenses nothing; cancel → full refund.<br><b>Decision combinations:</b> conditions such as <i>enough money?</i>, <i>product in stock?</i> and <i>change available?</i> form a decision table. It exposes cases like “enough money, in stock, but no change” — the machine should show “exact amount only” rather than keep the extra money.<br><b>Negative:</b> fake or foreign coins, torn notes, pressing two buttons at once, paying for a sold-out item.<br><b>Failures:</b> power cut after payment but before dispensing (neither money nor product may be lost), product stuck, card payment declined or timed out.<br><b>Usability and accessibility:</b> readable prices, reachable buttons, clear messages.<br><b>Operator side:</b> restocking, changing prices, cash collection and sales reports.<br><br>Prioritise money handling — no loss for the customer or the owner — and correct dispensing.',
    followups: ['Draw a decision table for payment, stock and change.', 'How would you test the card-payment part?'],
    mistakes: ['Ignoring change-making and refund scenarios.', 'Forgetting the operator side, such as restocking and price changes.'],
    lesson: 'mt-decision-tables'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-scn-06', cat: 'scenario', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'very-common',
    q: 'How would you test an e-commerce checkout flow?',
    short: 'Clarify payment methods, shipping, tax and promotions; then test cart-to-order end to end, calculations, validation and failures, duplicate-order protection, data persistence, security, performance at peak and compatibility.',
    answer: '<b>Clarify:</b> guest or logged-in checkout, payment methods (card, wallet, cash on delivery), shipping options, tax rules, coupons, when stock is reserved.<br><br><b>End to end:</b> cart → address → shipping → payment → confirmation; the order appears in “My orders” and the confirmation email or SMS arrives.<br><b>Calculations:</b> subtotal, discount, shipping, tax and total; rounding; currency.<br><b>Negative:</b> invalid or declined card, expired coupon, missing address fields, item going out of stock during checkout, price changing while in the cart.<br><b>Reliability:</b> double-clicking “Place order” must not create two orders or two charges; Back or Refresh on the payment page; payment succeeds but the redirect fails.<br><b>Data:</b> check the order, payment status and stock through the API or a read-only query — the success page alone isn’t proof.<br><b>Security:</b> HTTPS, card data never stored or logged by us, price can’t be changed by editing the request.<br><b>Performance and compatibility:</b> sale-day load; mobile and main browsers.<br><br>Prioritise money and order integrity. Automate the happy path and key negative cases at API level, plus one UI end-to-end test.',
    followups: ['How would you check that a double click doesn’t create duplicate orders?', 'What would you verify in the database after an order is placed?', 'How do you test payments without charging real cards?'],
    mistakes: ['Treating the “Order placed” screen as proof without checking the order, payment and stock records.', 'Ignoring calculation, rounding and concurrency problems.'],
    lesson: 'fd-risk-based'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-scn-07', cat: 'scenario', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'common',
    q: 'How would you test a search box like the one on a big search engine?',
    short: 'Clarify what is searched and how results are ranked, then test relevance, exact and partial matches, autocomplete, empty and special-character input, very long queries, pagination, speed, injection safety, localisation and accessibility.',
    answer: '<b>Clarify:</b> what is searched (products, documents, the web)? Exact, partial or fuzzy matching? Autocomplete, filters, sorting? Target response time?<br><br><b>Functional:</b> an exact term returns the right item near the top; partial words, plurals, case-insensitivity, spelling mistakes (“did you mean…”), several words, quoted phrases; a helpful message when nothing is found.<br><b>Autocomplete:</b> suggestions appear after the agreed number of characters, update while typing, work with arrow keys, and selecting one runs the search.<br><b>Negative and boundary:</b> empty search, only spaces, very long text, emoji, Unicode and right-to-left text, characters such as <code>%</code>, <code>_</code> and <code>\'</code>.<br><b>Security:</b> input like <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> is shown as text, never executed; SQL characters cause no errors.<br><b>Performance:</b> results within the target time; autocomplete stays responsive when typing fast; behaviour under load.<br><b>Other:</b> pagination and result counts, Back button keeps the query, label and result announcements for screen readers, mobile layout.<br><br>Relevance is hard to automate fully: I’d automate known-answer queries and use exploratory sessions to judge ranking quality.',
    followups: ['How would you test search relevance?', 'What should happen if the search service is down?'],
    mistakes: ['Only checking that some results appear, not that the right ones appear in a sensible order.', 'Forgetting special characters, empty input and response time.'],
    lesson: 'wm-forms-ui'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-scn-08', cat: 'scenario', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'common',
    q: 'How would you test a file upload feature?',
    short: 'Clarify allowed types, size limits and storage; then test valid uploads, size and type boundaries, renamed or corrupted files, interrupted and parallel uploads, error messages, server-side validation, access control and accessibility.',
    answer: '<b>Clarify:</b> allowed formats (e.g. PDF and DOCX for a CV), maximum size, single or multiple files, drag-and-drop, where files are stored, who may download them.<br><br><b>Functional:</b> upload each allowed type; the file appears with the right name and size; download it and confirm it is identical to the original; replace and delete work.<br><b>Boundary:</b> a file exactly at the size limit and just above; a 0-byte file; very long file names; names with spaces, Unicode or special characters.<br><b>Negative:</b> unsupported types; an <code>.exe</code> renamed to <code>.pdf</code> (the server should check content, not just the extension); corrupted or password-protected files.<br><b>Reliability:</b> network drop mid-upload, cancel, slow connection with a progress bar, uploading the same file twice, many users uploading at once.<br><b>Security:</b> validation happens on the server too; virus scanning if required; uploaded files can’t be executed or opened by other users through a guessed URL.<br><b>UI and accessibility:</b> clear errors, keyboard-accessible button, camera or gallery upload on mobile.<br><br>Automate type and size rules at API level; keep drag-and-drop and visual checks for a few UI tests.',
    followups: ['How would you test the size limit without creating huge files by hand?', 'Why is browser-side validation alone not enough?'],
    mistakes: ['Testing only with small, valid files.', 'Trusting the file extension instead of checking that the server validates the real content.'],
    lesson: 'wm-forms-ui'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-scn-09', cat: 'scenario', level: 'advanced', freq: 'common',
    q: 'How would you test a payment gateway integration?',
    short: 'Use the provider’s sandbox and test cards to cover success, decline, OTP/3-D Secure, timeouts and retries; verify callbacks or webhooks, reconciliation between systems, refunds, idempotency and security — never use real cards in production for testing.',
    answer: '<b>Clarify:</b> which provider and methods (cards, mobile wallets, bank transfer), currencies, full and partial refunds, and how our system learns the result — redirect, webhook or polling.<br><br><b>Environment:</b> the provider’s sandbox with its documented test cards and accounts for success, decline, insufficient funds and authentication challenges.<br><b>Functional:</b> success marks the order “paid”; decline leaves it unpaid with a clear message; OTP/3-D Secure pass and fail; refunds update both systems.<br><b>Failures and reliability (highest risk):</b> gateway timeout; user closes the browser right after paying; webhook arrives late, twice or out of order; a retry with the same idempotency key must not charge twice.<br><b>Reconciliation:</b> amount, currency and status match between our database, the gateway dashboard and what the user sees.<br><b>Security:</b> we never store or log full card data; the amount can’t be tampered with in the request; webhook signatures are verified; HTTPS everywhere.<br><br><b>Automation:</b> API tests against a mocked gateway for hard-to-trigger edge cases, plus a few end-to-end runs in the sandbox before each release.',
    followups: ['How would you test a webhook that arrives twice?', 'The user was charged but the order shows unpaid — how would you investigate?'],
    mistakes: ['Testing only the success path with a single test card.', 'Forgetting callbacks, timeouts and reconciliation between systems.'],
    lesson: 'sa-mocking'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-scn-10', cat: 'scenario', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'common',
    q: 'How would you test a mobile chat (messaging) app?',
    short: 'Clarify the features, then test sending and receiving with status indicators, offline and weak-network behaviour, message order, notifications, interruptions, permissions, media, multi-device sync, battery and data use, privacy and performance.',
    answer: '<b>Clarify:</b> one-to-one and group chats? Media and voice notes, read receipts, end-to-end encryption, multi-device login, deleting messages?<br><br><b>Functional:</b> send and receive text, emoji and media; sent/delivered/read indicators; create groups, add and remove members; delete for me vs for everyone; search; block a contact.<br><b>Network (critical on mobile):</b> send while offline → queued and sent on reconnect; switch Wi-Fi ↔ mobile data mid-send; slow connections; no duplicated or lost messages; correct order.<br><b>Interruptions and lifecycle:</b> incoming call while recording a voice note; app in background or killed — notifications still arrive and open the right chat.<br><b>Permissions:</b> camera, microphone, contacts and notifications — denied, then granted later.<br><b>Compatibility:</b> Android and iOS versions, screen sizes, low-end phones.<br><b>Performance:</b> large groups, long histories, battery and data usage, app size.<br><b>Privacy:</b> message content not visible in server logs, logout clears local data.<br><br>Prioritise reliable delivery and ordering, then notifications and network handling. Automate core send/receive flows at API level and keep a real-device checklist for network and interruption cases.',
    followups: ['How would you test message ordering on a poor network?', 'How would you test push notifications?'],
    mistakes: ['Testing only on fast Wi-Fi with a perfect connection.', 'Forgetting the receiver’s side — notifications, status indicators and multi-device sync.'],
    lesson: 'wm-mobile-conditions'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-scn-11', cat: 'scenario', level: 'beginner', freq: 'occasional',
    q: 'How would you test a coffee machine?',
    short: 'Clarify the model and drink options, then test each drink against measurable criteria, option combinations, error states such as an empty tank, safety, cleaning cycles, usability and reliability.',
    answer: '<b>Clarify:</b> home or office machine? Which drinks, sizes, strength and sugar options? Pods, beans or powder? Any spec for temperature and volume?<br><br><b>Functional:</b> each button makes the right drink at the stated volume and temperature; options combine correctly (large + strong + no sugar). A decision table or pairwise approach keeps the combinations manageable.<br><b>States and errors:</b> water tank empty, beans or pods empty, waste tray full, no cup, door open, descaling due — each should block brewing with a clear message and recover once fixed.<br><b>Boundary:</b> minimum water level, many cups in a row, cancelling mid-brew.<br><b>Safety:</b> no hot-water splashing, outer surfaces safe to touch, automatic shut-off, electrical safety marks.<br><b>Usability:</b> clear icons and labels, language, time to make one cup.<br><b>Reliability:</b> hundreds of cups a day for an office model, consistent results over time, power cut during brewing.<br><br>Prioritise safety and correct drinks. Taste is subjective, so agree measurable criteria — temperature, volume, time — as the pass/fail rule.',
    followups: ['How would you reduce the number of option combinations to test?', 'What would you test first if you had only ten minutes?'],
    mistakes: ['Only checking that coffee comes out and ignoring error states such as an empty water tank.', 'Using personal taste as the pass/fail rule instead of measurable criteria.'],
    lesson: 'mt-decision-tables'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-scn-12', cat: 'scenario', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'common',
    q: 'How would you test a forgot-password (password reset) flow?',
    short: 'Test the whole flow — request, link or OTP, new password — plus link expiry and single use, password rules, no user enumeration, rate limiting, old sessions ended, and that the old password stops working.',
    answer: '<b>Clarify:</b> reset by email link or OTP? How long is the link valid? Password policy? Should other sessions be logged out?<br><br><b>Functional:</b> request a reset for a registered email → the message arrives with a working link; set a valid new password; log in with it; the old password no longer works.<br><b>Token rules:</b> the link expires after the stated time; it works only once; a newer request invalidates older links; a random or edited token is rejected; the link resets only the right account.<br><b>Negative:</b> a new password that breaks the policy, confirmation mismatch, reusing a recent password if that is forbidden.<br><b>Security:</b> the same response for registered and unregistered emails (no user enumeration); rate limiting on repeated requests; the token doesn’t leak into logs or third-party requests; HTTPS; existing sessions end after the reset; the owner gets a “your password was changed” notice.<br><b>UX:</b> clear messages, works in mobile email apps, OTP auto-fill.<br><br>Automate the token rules at API level — they carry the highest risk and are easy to break in later changes.',
    followups: ['How would you test link expiry without waiting 24 hours?', 'What should happen if a user requests two reset emails?'],
    mistakes: ['Stopping at “email received, password changed” without testing expiry, reuse and enumeration.', 'Forgetting to confirm that the old password and existing sessions stop working.'],
    lesson: 'qb-security'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-scn-13', cat: 'scenario', level: 'beginner', freq: 'occasional',
    q: 'How would you test a date picker?',
    short: 'Clarify format, allowed range and locale, then test picking and typing dates, min/max boundaries, month ends and leap years, invalid input, time zones, localisation, keyboard and screen-reader access, and browser or mobile differences.',
    answer: '<b>Clarify:</b> allowed range (no past dates for a booking, no future dates for a birth date), display format (DD/MM/YYYY or MM/DD/YYYY), can users type as well as click, time zone, first day of the week.<br><br><b>Functional:</b> picking a date fills the field in the right format; month and year navigation; today highlighted; clearing the value; the saved value matches what was picked.<br><b>Boundary:</b> the first and last allowed dates and one day outside each; 29 February in leap and non-leap years (2024 vs 2025; 2000 was a leap year, 1900 was not); 30- vs 31-day months; 31 December → 1 January.<br><b>Negative:</b> typed 31/02, 00/13, letters, partial dates, pasted text.<br><b>Time zones:</b> a date chosen late at night isn’t saved as the previous or next day; daylight-saving changes.<br><b>Localisation:</b> month names, week start, right-to-left layout.<br><b>Accessibility:</b> full keyboard use, a proper label, the screen reader announces the chosen date.<br><b>Compatibility:</b> native mobile pickers and different browsers render it differently.',
    followups: ['Which boundary dates would you pick for a booking window of the next 90 days?', 'How would you reproduce the time-zone problem?'],
    mistakes: ['Forgetting leap years and month-end boundaries.', 'Only clicking dates in the calendar and never typing invalid ones.'],
    lesson: 'mt-boundary'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-scn-14', cat: 'scenario', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'common',
    q: 'How would you test a cart discount rule such as “10% off orders over 5,000, one coupon per order”?',
    short: 'Clarify the exact rule first, then use boundary values around the threshold, a decision table for combined conditions, checks on calculation order and rounding, state changes after the coupon is applied, and tamper/concurrency tests at API level.',
    answer: '<b>Clarify the ambiguities:</b> “over 5,000” or “5,000 and above”? Before or after tax and shipping? Do sale items count? Can it combine with other offers? Is there a maximum discount?<br><br><b>Boundary values:</b> cart totals of 4,999, 5,000 and 5,001 (adjusted once the rule is confirmed), plus a tiny and a very large cart.<br><b>Decision table:</b> conditions such as <i>total above threshold</i>, <i>valid coupon</i>, <i>member</i>, <i>sale item in cart</i> — each combination maps to an expected discount, which quickly reveals missing rules.<br><b>Calculation:</b> order of operations (discount then tax, or the reverse), rounding to two decimals, per-item vs whole-cart discount.<br><b>State changes:</b> apply the coupon, then remove items so the total drops below the threshold — the discount must disappear; expiry dates; usage limits; applying the same coupon twice.<br><b>Security:</b> the discount can’t be changed by editing the request; a single-use code can’t be redeemed twice by sending two requests at the same moment.<br><br>I’d automate these as data-driven API or unit tests — they are precise, numerous and easy to break.',
    followups: ['Draw a decision table for this rule.', 'How would you test that a single-use coupon can’t be redeemed twice at the same time?'],
    mistakes: ['Testing one value above and one below without asking whether the boundary is inclusive.', 'Checking only the displayed total, not the calculation and order of operations.'],
    lesson: 'mt-decision-tables'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-scn-15', cat: 'scenario', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'very-common',
    q: 'How would you test a REST API you have never seen before, with little or no documentation?',
    short: 'Discover first (OpenAPI file, developers, the DevTools Network tab), map the endpoints in a Postman collection, then test happy paths, status codes, schema and data, negative input, auth, side effects in the database and basic response times.',
    answer: '<b>Discover:</b> look for an OpenAPI/Swagger file; ask developers or the product owner for example requests; watch the browser DevTools Network tab while using the UI; read existing logs and tests.<br><b>Map it:</b> list endpoints, methods, headers, auth type and request/response shapes. Building a Postman collection as I go turns my exploration into documentation.<br><br><b>Happy path:</b> each endpoint with valid data — status code, body, schema, headers, response time.<br><b>Negative:</b> missing or wrong fields, wrong types, boundary values, unknown ids, wrong method, malformed JSON → expect clear 4xx errors, never a 500.<br><b>Auth:</b> no token → 401; valid token but someone else’s resource → 403 or 404; expired token → 401.<br><b>Side effects:</b> after POST, PUT or DELETE, confirm with a GET or a read-only query that the data really changed — and nothing else did.<br><b>Non-functional basics:</b> rough response times, rate limits.<br><br>Prioritise endpoints by business risk, and raise open questions with the team instead of assuming that whatever the API returns today is correct.',
    followups: ['The API returns 200 but the data is wrong — what do you do?', 'How would you turn this exploration into an automated suite?'],
    mistakes: ['Testing only the happy path because there is no specification.', 'Assuming whatever the API returns today is the correct behaviour.'],
    lesson: 'sa-api-basics'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-scn-16', cat: 'scenario', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'very-common',
    q: 'You get a new “export report to PDF” feature with no written requirements and a demo tomorrow. How would you test it?',
    short: 'Collect implicit requirements quickly (product owner, developer, similar features, user expectations), confirm my assumptions in writing, then run time-boxed, risk-based exploratory sessions and report findings early, as questions where unclear.',
    answer: 'No document doesn’t mean no expectations — my first job is to find them.<br><br><b>1. Gather (30 minutes):</b> a quick chat with the product owner and developer: who uses the export, which data, how large, which formats and page sizes? Look at the design mock-up, similar exports in the product (e.g. the CSV export) and how competitors behave.<br><b>2. Write assumptions down:</b> five or six bullet points such as “exports the current filters”, “max 10,000 rows”, “includes the company logo” — and get a quick confirmation in chat. That becomes my test basis.<br><b>3. Explore by risk:</b> charters like “correct data and totals compared with the screen”, “large and empty reports”, “special characters, Urdu or Arabic text”, “permissions — can a viewer export salary data?”, “file opens in common PDF readers and on mobile”.<br><b>4. Report early:</b> share bugs and questions the same day so the demo isn’t a surprise, and note what I didn’t cover.<br><br>Afterwards, the confirmed notes become test cases and a regression check.',
    followups: ['How do you decide whether something is a bug when there are no requirements?', 'What is a test charter?'],
    mistakes: ['Saying “I can’t test without requirements” or waiting for a document.', 'Testing only against personal opinion without confirming any assumption with the team.'],
    lesson: 'mt-exploratory'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-scn-17', cat: 'scenario', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'very-common',
    q: 'The release goes out in two hours and you’ve only tested half of the new feature. What do you do?',
    short: 'Test the riskiest parts first (smoke, critical flows, what changed), use automation and teammates in parallel, and tell stakeholders early exactly what is tested, untested and risky so they can make an informed go/no-go decision — never sign off quietly.',
    answer: '<b>1. Get the facts in ten minutes:</b> what exactly changed, which defects are open, what has already passed.<br><b>2. Prioritise by risk:</b> a smoke test of the build, then the critical paths of the new feature (money, data, security, the main user journey), then areas touched by the change and recent fixes. Cosmetic checks wait.<br><b>3. Get help:</b> run the automated regression suite in parallel; ask a developer or another tester to cover some checks; use focused exploratory testing on the riskiest parts.<br><b>4. Communicate early — not at minute 119:</b> a short status with what is tested, what isn’t, open defects with severity, and the risk of releasing. Offer options: release as is, release with the feature behind a flag, or delay.<br><b>5. Decision:</b> the go/no-go belongs to the product owner or release manager. My job is an honest, evidence-based picture — not a silent sign-off.<br><br>After release, finish the remaining tests and raise the root cause (late build, unclear scope) in the retrospective.',
    followups: ['What if the manager wants to release despite a critical open bug?', 'How do you decide what goes into a smoke test?'],
    mistakes: ['Rushing through random test cases or quietly signing off to meet the deadline.', 'Claiming the tester alone decides whether the release goes ahead.'],
    lesson: 'qb-release'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-scn-18', cat: 'scenario', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'very-common',
    q: 'A critical bug is found in production that testing missed. What do you do?',
    short: 'Help contain it first: reproduce, gather evidence, assess impact and log it with the right severity; support the hotfix with retesting and focused regression. Then run a blameless root-cause analysis and close the gap with new tests or process changes.',
    answer: '<b>Immediate response:</b> reproduce it — carefully in production, or in staging with production-like data; collect evidence (steps, logs, screenshots, affected users, app and OS versions); log it with the right severity and priority; alert the team. Check whether a workaround, rollback or feature flag can limit the damage.<br><br><b>Fix:</b> retest the hotfix in staging, run focused regression around it, then verify in production after deployment with a smoke check and monitoring.<br><br><b>Learn (blameless):</b> why did it escape? A missing requirement, no test for that path, test data unlike production, an environment difference, a late change that skipped regression, or a known risk the team accepted? The “5 Whys” helps here.<br><br><b>Prevent:</b> add a test for the case (automated if possible), improve test data or environments, update checklists, add monitoring or alerts so similar failures are spotted sooner.<br><br>In the interview, stay calm and take ownership without blaming developers or yourself — the team ships together.',
    followups: ['How would you explain the miss to your manager?', 'What is a hotfix, and how is it tested?'],
    mistakes: ['Getting defensive or blaming the developers.', 'Fixing the bug but never asking why it escaped or adding a test for it.'],
    lesson: 'mt-bug-reports'
  },

  /* ===================== API testing ===================== */
  {
    id: 'iq-api-01', cat: 'api', level: 'beginner', freq: 'very-common',
    q: 'What is an API, and what makes an API RESTful?',
    short: 'An API is a defined contract that lets one program ask another for data or actions. A REST API exposes resources at URLs and uses standard HTTP methods, status codes and stateless requests, usually with JSON bodies.',
    answer: 'An <b>API</b> (Application Programming Interface) is an agreed way for software components to talk to each other — like a waiter who takes your order to the kitchen and brings back the food; you never need to know how the kitchen works.<br><br><b>REST</b> (Representational State Transfer) is an architectural style for web APIs:<br>• <b>Resources</b> identified by URLs, e.g. <code>/users/42</code>.<br>• <b>Standard HTTP methods</b> express the action: GET reads, POST creates, PUT/PATCH update, DELETE removes.<br>• <b>Stateless:</b> every request carries what the server needs (such as the auth token); the server doesn’t remember earlier requests.<br>• <b>Client–server</b> separation, <b>cacheable</b> responses and a <b>uniform interface</b>.<br>• Results are signalled with <b>status codes</b>, and bodies are usually <b>JSON</b>.<br><br><b>API testing</b> means calling these endpoints directly — checking status codes, data, schema, errors, security and performance without the UI. It runs faster and breaks less often than UI testing and finds problems earlier, which is why most strategies put many tests at the API layer.',
    followups: ['What is the difference between API testing and UI testing?', 'What does “stateless” mean in practice?'],
    mistakes: ['Calling REST a protocol, or saying REST must use JSON.', 'Defining an API only as “a URL that returns data”.'],
    lesson: 'sa-api-basics'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-api-02', cat: 'api', level: 'beginner', freq: 'very-common',
    q: 'What are the main HTTP methods, and which of them are idempotent?',
    short: 'GET reads, POST creates or triggers an action, PUT replaces, PATCH partially updates and DELETE removes. GET, HEAD, OPTIONS, PUT and DELETE are idempotent; POST is not, and PATCH is not guaranteed to be.',
    answer: '<b>GET</b> — read a resource; must not change data (a <i>safe</i> method).<br><b>POST</b> — create a resource or trigger an action (<code>POST /orders</code>).<br><b>PUT</b> — create or fully replace the resource at a known URL.<br><b>PATCH</b> — change some fields only.<br><b>DELETE</b> — remove a resource.<br><b>HEAD</b> (headers only) and <b>OPTIONS</b> (allowed methods, CORS preflight) also come up.<br><br><b>Idempotent</b> means sending the same request once or ten times leaves the server in the same state. GET, HEAD, OPTIONS, PUT and DELETE are idempotent. <b>POST is not</b> — ten POSTs may create ten orders. <b>PATCH is not guaranteed</b> — “set status to shipped” is repeatable, “add 1 to quantity” is not.<br><br>Idempotency is about server <i>state</i>, not the response: a second DELETE may return 404 instead of 204, yet the end state (resource gone) is the same.<br><br><b>How I test it:</b> repeat the request and compare the resulting data with a GET or a read-only query; for POST, check how retries and double-clicks are handled, for example with an idempotency key.',
    followups: ['Is a safe method the same as an idempotent one?', 'How would you test that a payment POST doesn’t charge twice on retry?'],
    mistakes: ['Saying PATCH is always idempotent, or that DELETE isn’t idempotent because the second call returns 404.', 'Confusing idempotent with “returns exactly the same response every time”.'],
    lesson: 'sa-api-basics'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-api-03', cat: 'api', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'very-common',
    q: 'What is the difference between PUT, PATCH and POST?',
    short: 'POST creates a new resource (the server usually assigns the id) and isn’t idempotent. PUT replaces the whole resource at a known URL and is idempotent. PATCH changes only the fields sent and isn’t guaranteed to be idempotent.',
    answer: 'Take the user <code>{ "name": "Ali", "city": "Lahore", "role": "tester" }</code> stored at <code>/users/7</code>.<br><br><b>POST /users</b> with a body creates a new user. The server usually generates the id and returns <b>201 Created</b>, often with a <code>Location</code> header. Sending it twice normally creates two users — <b>not idempotent</b>.<br><br><b>PUT /users/7</b> with <code>{ "name": "Ali", "city": "Karachi" }</code> <b>replaces</b> the resource. Strictly, fields you leave out (role) may be removed or reset, so clients send the full representation. Repeating it gives the same result — <b>idempotent</b>.<br><br><b>PATCH /users/7</b> with <code>{ "city": "Karachi" }</code> changes only the city; name and role stay. It is <b>not guaranteed idempotent</b> — an “append to list” patch adds again each time.<br><br>Real APIs don’t always follow these rules strictly, so I check the documentation. <b>What I test:</b> after PUT, omitted fields behave as documented; after PATCH, untouched fields are unchanged; for POST, duplicates and retries — and always a follow-up GET, because a 200 or 201 alone doesn’t prove the data was saved correctly.',
    followups: ['Which status codes would you expect for each method?', 'What should happen if you PUT to an id that doesn’t exist?'],
    mistakes: ['Saying PUT and PATCH are the same thing.', 'Forgetting that PUT replaces the whole resource and that POST is not idempotent.'],
    lesson: 'sa-api-basics'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-api-04', cat: 'api', level: 'beginner', freq: 'very-common',
    q: 'Which HTTP status codes do you know, and what do they mean?',
    short: '2xx success (200, 201, 204), 3xx redirection, 4xx client errors (400, 401, 403, 404, 409, 422, 429) and 5xx server errors (500, 502, 503). As a tester I check that the code is the right one, not just that it isn’t a 500.',
    answer: '<b>2xx success:</b> <b>200 OK</b> (general success), <b>201 Created</b> (new resource, usually after POST), <b>204 No Content</b> (success with no body, common for DELETE).<br><b>3xx redirection:</b> 301/302 moved, 304 not modified (caching).<br><b>4xx client errors:</b> <b>400 Bad Request</b> (malformed or invalid input); <b>401 Unauthorized</b> (not authenticated — credentials missing, invalid or expired); <b>403 Forbidden</b> (authenticated but not allowed); <b>404 Not Found</b> (no such resource — sometimes used deliberately to hide one); 405 method not allowed; <b>409 Conflict</b> (clashes with the current state, e.g. a duplicate email); <b>422 Unprocessable Content</b> (well-formed but fails validation rules); <b>429 Too Many Requests</b> (rate limit reached).<br><b>5xx server errors:</b> <b>500 Internal Server Error</b> (unhandled failure); <b>502 Bad Gateway</b> (a proxy or gateway got an invalid response from the server behind it); <b>503 Service Unavailable</b> (overloaded or in maintenance); 504 gateway timeout.<br><br><b>Testing tips:</b> bad user input should never produce a 5xx — that usually means missing validation. And a 2xx alone doesn’t prove the result is correct; check the body and persistence too.',
    followups: ['When would you expect 409 rather than 400?', 'What does a 502 tell you during testing?', 'Which status should a successful DELETE return?'],
    mistakes: ['Mixing up 401 and 403.', 'Treating any 2xx as a pass without checking the body and side effects.'],
    lesson: 'sa-api-basics'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-api-05', cat: 'api', level: 'beginner', freq: 'very-common',
    q: 'What is the difference between 401 and 403?',
    short: '401 Unauthorized means the server doesn’t know who you are — credentials are missing, invalid or expired. 403 Forbidden means you are authenticated but not allowed to do this. Logging in again can fix a 401, never a 403.',
    answer: 'Despite its name, <b>401 Unauthorized</b> is about <b>authentication</b>: no token, a malformed token, a wrong password or an expired session. The fix is to (re)authenticate. The response should include a <code>WWW-Authenticate</code> header describing how.<br><br><b>403 Forbidden</b> is about <b>authorization</b>: the server knows who you are, but your role or permissions don’t allow the action — for example, a normal PeopleOps employee calling <code>DELETE /admin/users/5</code>. Logging in again won’t help.<br><br>Some APIs deliberately return <b>404</b> instead of 403 for resources you shouldn’t even know exist, such as another customer’s order, so an attacker can’t confirm that the id is valid.<br><br><b>Tests I’d write:</b><br>• no token → 401; expired or tampered token → 401;<br>• valid token with an insufficient role → 403;<br>• user A requests user B’s resource → 403 or 404 as designed, never 200;<br>• an admin sending the same request → success.<br>Also check that error bodies don’t leak sensitive details.',
    followups: ['How would you test authorization across three user roles?', 'Why might an API return 404 instead of 403?'],
    mistakes: ['Reversing them — saying 401 means “no permission” and 403 means “not logged in”.', 'Not realising that 401 is about authentication despite its name.'],
    lesson: 'sa-auth'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-api-06', cat: 'api', level: 'beginner', freq: 'common',
    q: 'What are HTTP headers, and which ones do you check in API testing?',
    short: 'Headers are key–value metadata on requests and responses. Key ones: Content-Type (format of the body sent), Accept (format wanted back), Authorization (credentials); on responses also Location, Cache-Control, Set-Cookie and security headers.',
    answer: 'Headers carry information <i>about</i> the message rather than the data itself.<br><br><b>Request headers:</b><br>• <code>Content-Type: application/json</code> — the format of the body I’m sending.<br>• <code>Accept: application/json</code> — the format I want back.<br>• <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code> — credentials.<br>• custom headers such as <code>X-Request-Id</code> or an API-key header.<br><br><b>Response headers:</b> <code>Content-Type</code> (does it match the body?), <code>Location</code> after 201 Created, <code>Cache-Control</code>, <code>Set-Cookie</code> with <code>HttpOnly</code>, <code>Secure</code> and <code>SameSite</code> flags, <code>Retry-After</code> with 429 or 503, and security headers such as <code>Strict-Transport-Security</code>.<br><br><b>Tests:</b> send the wrong Content-Type (XML to a JSON-only endpoint) → expect 415 Unsupported Media Type or 400; request an unsupported Accept type → 406 or a documented default; omit Authorization → 401; confirm responses don’t reveal internal details such as server software versions.',
    followups: ['What is the difference between Content-Type and Accept?', 'Which status would you expect for an unsupported Content-Type?'],
    mistakes: ['Confusing Content-Type with Accept.', 'Ignoring response headers and checking only the body.'],
    lesson: 'sa-api-basics'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-api-07', cat: 'api', level: 'beginner', freq: 'common',
    q: 'What is the difference between path parameters and query parameters?',
    short: 'Path parameters are part of the URL path and identify a specific resource (/orders/1052). Query parameters come after the ? and filter, sort or paginate (/orders?status=shipped&page=2). Both need negative tests.',
    answer: '<b>Path parameter:</b> <code>GET /orders/1052</code> — <code>1052</code> identifies <i>which</i> resource. It is normally required; leaving it out changes the endpoint entirely.<br><b>Query parameters:</b> <code>GET /orders?status=shipped&amp;sort=date&amp;page=2</code> — optional key–value pairs after <code>?</code>, joined with <code>&amp;</code>, used to filter, sort, search or paginate.<br><b>Request body:</b> the data sent to create or update (POST/PUT/PATCH) — not part of the URL.<br><br><b>Path parameter tests:</b> valid id → 200; unknown id → 404; wrong type (<code>/orders/abc</code>) → 400 or 404 as documented, never 500; another user’s id → 403 or 404; zero, negative and very large numbers.<br><b>Query parameter tests:</b> each filter alone and combined; unknown or misspelled parameters (ignored or rejected?); invalid values such as <code>page=-1</code>; special characters URL-encoded correctly (a space as <code>%20</code>); defaults when parameters are omitted.<br><br>Sensitive data such as passwords or tokens shouldn’t travel in query strings — URLs end up in logs and browser history.',
    followups: ['Where would you send an API key — header, query string or body?', 'How should an API handle an unknown query parameter?'],
    mistakes: ['Mixing up path and query parameters.', 'Testing only valid values and never missing or malformed parameters.'],
    lesson: 'sa-api-basics'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-api-08', cat: 'api', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'very-common',
    q: 'Besides the status code, what do you validate in an API response?',
    short: 'The body data, the schema (fields, types, required), headers, response time, error format and side effects — confirming with a GET or a read-only DB query that the change really persisted and nothing else changed.',
    answer: 'A 200 or 201 only says the server <i>thinks</i> it succeeded. I check:<br>1. <b>Status code</b> — the exact one expected (201 for a create, not just “some 2xx”).<br>2. <b>Body data</b> — values match the request and business rules: right user, correct total, dates in the right format and time zone.<br>3. <b>Schema/contract</b> — required fields present, correct types, no unexpected nulls, valid enum values (JSON Schema validation helps).<br>4. <b>Headers</b> — <code>Content-Type</code>, <code>Location</code>, caching, cookie and security flags.<br>5. <b>Response time</b> — within the agreed limit.<br>6. <b>Side effects</b> — GET the resource or run a read-only SQL query to confirm it persisted; check related data (stock reduced, email queued, audit row written) and that nothing else changed.<br>7. <b>Error responses</b> — clear messages, consistent format, no stack traces.<br>8. <b>Data exposure</b> — no fields the caller shouldn’t see, such as password hashes or other users’ data.<br><br>Example: <code>POST /orders</code> returns 201, but a follow-up GET shows quantity 0 — the status passed, the feature failed.',
    followups: ['How do you validate a JSON schema in Postman or in code?', 'The API returns 200 but the data is wrong — what next?'],
    mistakes: ['Checking only the status code.', 'Assuming 201 Created proves the data was saved correctly.'],
    lesson: 'sa-api-negative'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-api-09', cat: 'api', level: 'beginner', freq: 'very-common',
    q: 'What is the difference between REST and SOAP?',
    short: 'SOAP is a protocol with strict XML envelopes, a WSDL contract and built-in WS-* standards. REST is an architectural style using URLs, HTTP methods and status codes, usually with JSON — lighter and the default for modern web and mobile APIs.',
    answer: '<b>Nature:</b> SOAP is a <i>protocol</i>; REST is an <i>architectural style</i>.<br><b>Format:</b> SOAP messages are always XML inside an Envelope (optional Header plus Body); REST usually uses JSON but can return XML, text or files.<br><b>Contract:</b> SOAP services are described by a <b>WSDL</b>; REST APIs are often described by an <b>OpenAPI/Swagger</b> spec (optional).<br><b>Operations:</b> SOAP typically sends POST requests to one endpoint and names the operation inside the XML; REST uses different URLs and HTTP methods.<br><b>Errors:</b> SOAP returns a <b>Fault</b> element; REST relies on HTTP status codes plus an error body.<br><b>Standards:</b> SOAP has WS-Security and related standards, so it remains common in banking, telecoms and older enterprise systems. REST is lighter, cache-friendly and the norm for web and mobile apps.<br><br><b>Testing:</b> for SOAP, validate the XML against the WSDL/XSD and check Fault handling (SoapUI or Postman); for REST, check status codes, JSON body and schema (Postman, REST Assured, Playwright’s request API).',
    followups: ['Can a REST API return XML?', 'Which one would you expect in an older banking integration, and why?'],
    mistakes: ['Saying REST is always more secure, or that SOAP is obsolete and never used.', 'Claiming REST can only use JSON.'],
    lesson: 'sa-rest-soap'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-api-10', cat: 'api', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'occasional',
    q: 'What is GraphQL, and how is testing it different from testing REST?',
    short: 'GraphQL is a query language for APIs: clients send queries or mutations to one endpoint and ask for exactly the fields they need. Testing focuses on the schema, errors in the body (often with HTTP 200), field-level authorization and depth limits.',
    answer: 'GraphQL usually exposes <b>one endpoint</b> (e.g. <code>POST /graphql</code>). The client sends a <b>query</b> (read) or <b>mutation</b> (write) naming exactly the fields it wants, and a strongly typed <b>schema</b> defines every type and operation.<br><br>Example: <code>query { user(id: 7) { name orders { id total } } }</code> returns only the name and orders — no over- or under-fetching.<br><br><b>What changes for testers:</b><br>• <b>Status codes say less:</b> many servers return HTTP 200 even when part of the query fails, with an <code>errors</code> array in the body — so always assert on <code>errors</code> and <code>data</code>.<br>• <b>Schema:</b> types, nullability, deprecated fields; introspection is often disabled in production.<br>• <b>Authorization per field and object:</b> can a normal user request <code>salary</code>, or another user’s data, through a nested query?<br>• <b>Abuse:</b> deeply nested or huge queries should hit depth or complexity limits instead of slowing the server.<br>• <b>Variables and mutations:</b> invalid input types; side effects confirmed with a follow-up query.<br><br>Postman, Insomnia, Playwright and REST Assured can all send GraphQL requests.',
    followups: ['Why might a failed GraphQL query still return HTTP 200?', 'What is introspection, and should it be enabled in production?'],
    mistakes: ['Testing GraphQL only by looking at the HTTP status code.', 'Assuming one endpoint means authorization only needs to be tested once.'],
    lesson: 'sa-rest-soap'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-api-11', cat: 'api', level: 'beginner', freq: 'very-common',
    q: 'In Postman, what are collections, environments and variables?',
    short: 'A collection groups related requests, with their scripts, into folders you can run together. An environment is a named set of variables such as baseUrl and token for Dev, QA or Staging. Variables, written {{name}}, avoid hard-coding and have scopes.',
    answer: '<b>Collection:</b> a saved, shareable group of requests organised in folders (Auth, Users, Orders) with their tests and scripts. It can be run with the Collection Runner or from the command line with <b>Newman</b>, for example in a CI pipeline.<br><br><b>Environment:</b> a named set of key–value pairs — <code>baseUrl</code>, <code>username</code>, <code>token</code> — so the same collection runs against Dev, QA or Staging just by switching the environment.<br><br><b>Variables</b> are referenced as <code>{{baseUrl}}/users</code>. Scopes from broadest to narrowest: <b>global</b>, <b>collection</b>, <b>environment</b>, <b>data</b> (from a CSV/JSON file during a run) and <b>local</b> (inside a script). If the same name exists at several levels, the narrowest scope wins.<br><br><b>Good practice:</b> keep secrets in environment variables marked as secret and out of Git; never hard-code ids — capture them from responses; and keep the collection runnable from a clean state so it behaves the same on a teammate’s machine and in CI.',
    followups: ['How would you run a Postman collection in a CI pipeline?', 'If a variable exists in both the environment and global scope, which value is used?'],
    mistakes: ['Hard-coding URLs and tokens into every request.', 'Not knowing the variable scopes or which one takes precedence.'],
    lesson: 'sa-postman'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-api-12', cat: 'api', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'common',
    q: 'How do you write tests and pre-request scripts in Postman?',
    short: 'Pre-request scripts run before the request (generate data, set timestamps, fetch a token). Post-response test scripts use pm.test with pm.expect to assert on status, body, headers and time, and save values for later requests.',
    answer: 'Postman scripts are JavaScript using the <code>pm</code> object; newer versions show them under <b>Scripts → Pre-request</b> and <b>Post-response</b> (formerly the “Tests” tab).<br><br><b>Pre-request script</b> — runs before sending, e.g. to create a unique email:<br><code>pm.environment.set("email", "qa" + Date.now() + "@example.com");</code><br><br><b>Post-response script</b> — runs after the response arrives:<br><code>pm.test("201 Created", () => pm.response.to.have.status(201));</code><br><code>const body = pm.response.json();</code><br><code>pm.test("id is a number", () => pm.expect(body.id).to.be.a("number"));</code><br><code>pm.test("fast enough", () => pm.expect(pm.response.responseTime).to.be.below(800));</code><br><code>pm.environment.set("userId", body.id);</code><br><br>Each <code>pm.test</code> appears as pass or fail in the response panel, the Collection Runner and Newman reports. Scripts can also live at folder or collection level, so shared checks such as response time or Content-Type run for every request.<br><br>Keep assertions specific — a test that only checks “status is 200” proves very little.',
    followups: ['How would you validate a JSON schema in a Postman test?', 'How do you run the same request with data from a CSV file?'],
    mistakes: ['Writing only a status-code check and calling it a test.', 'Copying the same assertions into every request instead of using folder or collection scripts.'],
    lesson: 'sa-postman'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-api-13', cat: 'api', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'very-common',
    q: 'What is API chaining, and how do you do it in Postman?',
    short: 'API chaining means using data from one response in the next request — log in and save the token, create an order and save its id, then GET or DELETE that order. In Postman you store values in variables from a post-response script.',
    answer: 'Real flows depend on earlier steps, so tests pass data along a chain:<br>1. <code>POST /login</code> → post-response script: <code>pm.environment.set("token", pm.response.json().token);</code><br>2. <code>POST /orders</code> with the header <code>Authorization: Bearer {{token}}</code> → save <code>orderId</code> from the response.<br>3. <code>GET /orders/{{orderId}}</code> → assert the order exists with the right data.<br>4. <code>DELETE /orders/{{orderId}}</code> → 204; GET again → 404.<br><br>Run the folder in order with the Collection Runner or Newman. In code — REST Assured, Playwright or Python requests — it is the same idea: extract a value from one response and pass it into the next call.<br><br><b>Good practice:</b> each chain creates its own data instead of relying on ids that exist only on one machine; authenticate once (a collection-level pre-request script or a setup step); clean up what you created; and stop with a clear message when an early step fails, rather than letting later steps produce confusing errors.',
    followups: ['How would you handle a token that expires during a long run?', 'Why is it risky for every test to depend on the previous one?'],
    mistakes: ['Copying tokens and ids between requests by hand.', 'Building one long chain where a single failure hides the results of everything after it.'],
    lesson: 'sa-postman'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-api-14', cat: 'api', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'common',
    q: 'What types of API authentication have you worked with?',
    short: 'Common types: Basic auth (Base64 of username:password, safe only over HTTPS), API keys, Bearer tokens (often JWTs) and OAuth 2.0, where an authorization server issues scoped access tokens through flows like authorization code or client credentials.',
    answer: '<b>Basic auth:</b> <code>Authorization: Basic base64(username:password)</code>. Base64 is encoding, not encryption, so it must only travel over HTTPS.<br><b>API key:</b> a long secret identifying the calling application, ideally sent in a header such as <code>x-api-key</code> rather than the query string (URLs end up in logs). It usually identifies the app, not the user.<br><b>Bearer token:</b> <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code> — whoever holds the token gets access, so tokens expire. Often a <b>JWT</b> issued after login.<br><b>OAuth 2.0:</b> an authorization framework in which an authorization server issues access tokens (and often refresh tokens) limited by <b>scopes</b>. Common flows: <b>authorization code with PKCE</b> for users signing in through web or mobile apps, and <b>client credentials</b> for service-to-service calls. Social sign-in buttons usually add <b>OpenID Connect</b> on top for identity.<br><br><b>Tests for any type:</b> no credentials → 401; wrong or expired credentials → 401; valid credentials without the needed permission or scope → 403; revoked keys and tokens stop working; refresh works; secrets never appear in URLs, logs or error messages.',
    followups: ['What is the difference between OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect?', 'How would you test token expiry and refresh?'],
    mistakes: ['Calling Base64 encryption.', 'Describing OAuth 2.0 as a login protocol and mixing up authentication with authorization.'],
    lesson: 'sa-auth'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-api-15', cat: 'api', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'common',
    q: 'What is a JWT, what does it contain, and what would you test?',
    short: 'A JSON Web Token has three Base64URL parts — header.payload.signature. The header names the algorithm, the payload holds claims (sub, exp, role) and the signature reveals tampering. It is signed, not encrypted, so anyone can read the payload.',
    answer: 'Structure: <code>xxxxx.yyyyy.zzzzz</code><br>• <b>Header</b> — token type and signing algorithm, e.g. <code>{"alg":"HS256","typ":"JWT"}</code>.<br>• <b>Payload</b> — <i>claims</i>: <code>sub</code> (user id), <code>exp</code> (expiry), <code>iat</code> (issued at), <code>iss</code>, <code>aud</code>, plus custom ones such as role.<br>• <b>Signature</b> — calculated over header and payload with a secret or private key, so the server can detect any change.<br><br>Each part is Base64URL-encoded, <b>not encrypted</b> — paste a token into a decoder and you can read it. So a JWT must never carry passwords or sensitive personal data.<br><br><b>What I test:</b><br>• expired token (<code>exp</code> in the past) → 401;<br>• payload edited (role changed to admin) without re-signing → 401;<br>• <code>alg</code> changed to <code>none</code> or another algorithm → rejected;<br>• a token from another environment or with the wrong <code>aud</code> → rejected;<br>• role claims really enforced (403 for forbidden actions);<br>• logout, revocation and refresh-token behaviour;<br>• tokens never placed in URLs or stored where injected scripts can easily read them.',
    followups: ['Why can’t a user simply change the role in the payload?', 'Where should a web app store a JWT, and what are the trade-offs?'],
    mistakes: ['Saying a JWT is encrypted and therefore safe for sensitive data.', 'Never testing expired or tampered tokens.'],
    lesson: 'sa-auth'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-api-16', cat: 'api', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'very-common',
    q: 'What negative test cases would you write for a POST /users API?',
    short: 'Missing or empty fields, wrong types, invalid formats, boundary lengths, duplicates, malformed JSON, wrong Content-Type or method, missing or expired auth, wrong role, injection strings, huge payloads — expecting clear 4xx errors and nothing saved.',
    answer: 'Assume the body has <code>name</code>, <code>email</code> and <code>age</code>.<br><br><b>Validation (expect 400 or 422):</b> each required field missing; empty strings; <code>null</code>; wrong types (<code>"age": "twenty"</code>); invalid email formats; lengths and ages at min−1 and max+1; unknown extra fields (ignored or rejected, as documented).<br><b>Structure:</b> malformed JSON → 400; empty body; wrong <code>Content-Type</code> → 415; wrong method (<code>PUT /users</code>) → 405.<br><b>Business rules:</b> duplicate email → 409 or the documented code.<br><b>Auth:</b> no token → 401; expired token → 401; a non-admin creating users → 403.<br><b>Robustness and security:</b> SQL or script injection strings stored safely as plain text; a very large payload → 413 or 400; Unicode and special characters in names handled.<br><br><b>For every negative case also check:</b> the error message is clear and leaks no stack trace — and, the part people forget, <b>no user was created</b> (verify with a GET or a read-only query).<br><br>In automation I drive these from a data table, so each new case is one row.',
    followups: ['Which of these cases should never return a 500?', 'How would you organise these as data-driven tests?'],
    mistakes: ['Checking only the status code without confirming that nothing was saved.', 'Testing only invalid emails and ignoring auth, structure and business rules.'],
    lesson: 'sa-api-negative'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-api-17', cat: 'api', level: 'advanced', freq: 'occasional',
    q: 'What is contract testing, and how is it different from functional API testing?',
    short: 'Contract testing checks that an API provider and its consumers agree on the request and response format — fields, types, status codes. Consumer-driven tools like Pact record what consumers need and verify the provider against it.',
    answer: 'In microservices, the ShopFlow mobile app (consumer) depends on the Orders API (provider). If the provider renames <code>total</code> to <code>amount</code>, the app breaks — even though the provider’s own functional tests still pass.<br><br><b>Contract testing</b> pins down that agreement:<br>• <b>Spec-based:</b> validate every response against an OpenAPI spec or JSON Schema in each build.<br>• <b>Consumer-driven</b> (e.g. Pact): the consumer’s tests generate a contract listing the requests it makes and the fields it relies on; the provider’s pipeline replays them and fails if they’re no longer satisfied. A broker shares contracts and can tell you whether a version is safe to deploy.<br><br><b>Difference from functional testing:</b> functional tests check behaviour and business rules (“the discount is applied correctly”); contract tests check <i>shape and compatibility</i> (field exists, is a number, unknown id gives 404). They run fast, don’t need both services deployed together and reduce reliance on slow end-to-end suites.<br><br>Typical breaking changes they catch: removed or renamed fields, changed types, new required request fields, changed status codes.',
    followups: ['Is adding a new optional response field a breaking change?', 'Where do contract tests run in a CI pipeline?'],
    mistakes: ['Thinking contract testing replaces functional or end-to-end testing.', 'Describing it as just checking the status code.'],
    lesson: 'sa-api-negative'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-api-18', cat: 'api', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'common',
    q: 'How do you test an API that depends on another service that isn’t ready or is unreliable?',
    short: 'Replace the dependency with a stub or mock returning controlled responses — success, errors, timeouts, odd data — via Postman mock servers, WireMock or Playwright route interception, then run integration tests against the real service when it’s ready.',
    answer: '<b>Stub:</b> a simple stand-in returning canned responses (“GET /rates always returns 280.5”). <b>Mock:</b> similar, but also lets you verify how it was called — once, with the right body? <b>Service virtualisation</b> is the heavier, shared version for whole teams.<br><br><b>Why use them:</b> the dependency isn’t built yet, costs money per call (SMS, payments), is slow or flaky, or you need responses that are hard to trigger for real — 500s, timeouts, rate limits, malformed JSON, empty lists.<br><br><b>Tools:</b> Postman mock servers, WireMock, MockServer, Prism (generated from an OpenAPI file), and in UI tests Playwright’s <code>page.route()</code> to intercept network calls.<br><br><b>What I test with a mock:</b> our service handles the dependency’s errors gracefully (retries, clear message, no crash), times out sensibly, and doesn’t duplicate charges or records on retry.<br><br><b>Limit:</b> a mock only reflects what <i>we believe</i> the dependency does. Keep it aligned with the real contract (contract tests help) and still run a smaller set of integration tests against the real service before release.',
    followups: ['What is the difference between a mock and a stub?', 'What risk do mocks introduce, and how do you reduce it?'],
    mistakes: ['Waiting for the dependency to be ready before starting any testing.', 'Relying only on mocks and never testing the real integration.'],
    lesson: 'sa-mocking'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-api-19', cat: 'api', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'occasional',
    q: 'What is rate limiting, and how would you test it?',
    short: 'Rate limiting caps how many requests a client can make in a time window. Test that requests within the limit succeed, the next gets 429 Too Many Requests (often with Retry-After), the limit resets after the window and applies per user or key.',
    answer: 'APIs limit requests — say 100 per minute per API key — to protect against abuse, brute-force attacks and overload.<br><br><b>Test approach:</b><br>1. <b>Confirm the rule:</b> the limit, the window (fixed or sliding) and the scope (per user, IP, key or endpoint).<br>2. <b>Within the limit:</b> N requests all succeed.<br>3. <b>At the boundary:</b> request N+1 → <b>429 Too Many Requests</b> with a clear error body and headers such as <code>Retry-After</code> (and rate-limit headers if the API uses them).<br>4. <b>Reset:</b> after the window, requests succeed again.<br>5. <b>Isolation:</b> user A hitting the limit doesn’t block user B.<br>6. <b>Bypass attempts:</b> spoofing <code>X-Forwarded-For</code>, changing URL case, spreading requests across several tokens.<br>7. <b>Sensitive endpoints:</b> login, OTP and password reset should have stricter limits.<br><br>Use a script or a small load tool (a Postman loop, k6, JMeter) rather than clicking by hand, and run it in a test environment — never against production or third-party APIs without permission.',
    followups: ['How would you test a sliding-window limit?', 'Why are login and OTP endpoints so important to rate-limit?'],
    mistakes: ['Expecting 403 or 500 instead of 429 when the limit is exceeded.', 'Hammering a production or third-party API to test the limit without permission.'],
    lesson: 'sa-api-negative'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-api-20', cat: 'api', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'occasional',
    q: 'How would you test API pagination?',
    short: 'Check defaults and page-size limits, first, last, empty and out-of-range pages, invalid parameters, that no items are duplicated or skipped across pages, totals and next/previous links, a stable sort order, and behaviour when data changes between requests.',
    answer: 'Two common styles: <b>offset/limit</b> (<code>?page=3&amp;size=20</code>) and <b>cursor-based</b> (<code>?cursor=abc123</code>, with the next cursor returned in each response).<br><br><b>Tests:</b><br>• <b>Defaults:</b> no parameters → the documented default page size.<br>• <b>Boundaries:</b> size=1, the maximum size, above the maximum (capped or 400?), size=0, negative values, non-numbers.<br>• <b>Pages:</b> first, middle and last; a page beyond the last → empty list rather than an error, unless documented otherwise.<br>• <b>Completeness:</b> walk every page and confirm the combined items match the total with <b>no duplicates and no gaps</b> — compare with a read-only <code>COUNT(*)</code>.<br>• <b>Metadata:</b> <code>total</code>, <code>totalPages</code> and next/previous links are correct; the last page has no next link.<br>• <b>Ordering:</b> stable sorting (a unique tie-breaker such as id); filters and pagination together.<br>• <b>Data changes:</b> insert or delete records between page calls — offset paging can skip or repeat items, cursor paging shouldn’t.<br>• <b>Performance:</b> very deep pages still respond in time.',
    followups: ['Why can offset pagination show duplicates when data changes?', 'How would you automate the completeness check?'],
    mistakes: ['Testing only page 1 with the default size.', 'Never checking for duplicated or missing items across pages.'],
    lesson: 'sa-api-negative'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-api-21', cat: 'api', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'common',
    q: 'What is REST Assured, and what does a basic test look like?',
    short: 'REST Assured is a Java library for testing REST APIs in a readable given–when–then style. You set up the request (base URI, headers, body), send it, then assert status, headers and body with matchers, usually inside JUnit or TestNG.',
    answer: 'REST Assured is a Java DSL for API testing, normally run with JUnit or TestNG and built with Maven or Gradle.<br><br><code>given()</code><br><code>  .baseUri("https://api.shopflow.test")</code><br><code>  .header("Authorization", "Bearer " + token)</code><br><code>  .contentType(ContentType.JSON)</code><br><code>  .body(Map.of("name", "Sara", "role", "tester"))</code><br><code>.when()</code><br><code>  .post("/users")</code><br><code>.then()</code><br><code>  .statusCode(201)</code><br><code>  .body("name", equalTo("Sara"))</code><br><code>  .time(lessThan(2000L));</code><br><br><b>given</b> = preconditions (headers, auth, body, parameters); <b>when</b> = the HTTP call; <b>then</b> = assertions. <code>equalTo</code> and <code>lessThan</code> are Hamcrest matchers, and paths like <code>"items[0].id"</code> reach nested JSON fields.<br><br>Other useful features: <code>.extract().path("id")</code> to chain requests; request and response <b>specifications</b> to reuse common setup; JSON Schema validation; <code>.log().all()</code> for debugging; and serialising Java objects (POJOs) instead of building JSON strings by hand.<br><br>In JavaScript stacks, Playwright’s <code>request</code> fixture or Supertest play the same role.',
    followups: ['How do you extract a value from a response and use it in the next request?', 'What are request specifications used for?'],
    mistakes: ['Hard-coding base URLs and tokens in every test instead of using specifications or configuration.', 'Asserting only the status code.'],
    lesson: 'au-bdd-api-visual'
  },

  /* ===================== SQL and databases ===================== */
  {
    id: 'iq-sql-01', cat: 'sql', level: 'beginner', freq: 'common',
    q: 'What is SQL, and what is a relational database (RDBMS)?',
    short: 'SQL is the standard language for querying and changing data in relational databases. An RDBMS (MySQL, PostgreSQL, SQL Server, Oracle) stores data in tables of rows and columns linked by keys, and enforces constraints and transactions.',
    answer: 'A <b>relational database</b> stores data in <b>tables</b> — rows (records) and columns (fields) — and links tables through <b>keys</b>: <code>orders.customer_id</code> points to <code>customers.id</code>. The <b>RDBMS</b> is the software that manages it and enforces rules such as data types, NOT NULL, UNIQUE, foreign keys and transactions. Examples: MySQL, PostgreSQL, SQL Server, Oracle, SQLite.<br><br><b>SQL</b> (Structured Query Language) is how we talk to it. Commands are usually grouped as:<br>• <b>DQL/DML</b> — <code>SELECT</code>, <code>INSERT</code>, <code>UPDATE</code>, <code>DELETE</code> (data).<br>• <b>DDL</b> — <code>CREATE</code>, <code>ALTER</code>, <code>DROP</code>, <code>TRUNCATE</code> (structure).<br>• <b>DCL</b> — <code>GRANT</code>, <code>REVOKE</code> (permissions).<br>• <b>TCL</b> — <code>COMMIT</code>, <code>ROLLBACK</code>, <code>SAVEPOINT</code> (transactions).<br><br>The core syntax is standard, but dialects differ — e.g. <code>LIMIT</code> in MySQL and PostgreSQL, <code>TOP</code> in SQL Server, <code>FETCH FIRST</code> in Oracle and standard SQL.<br><br><b>NoSQL</b> databases (document, key–value) use flexible schemas; testers meet both, but interviews mostly test SQL.',
    followups: ['What is the difference between DDL and DML?', 'Which database have you used, and how did you connect to it for testing?'],
    mistakes: ['Saying SQL is a database — it is a language.', 'Not knowing that syntax differs between database products.'],
    lesson: 'sa-sql-basics'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-sql-02', cat: 'sql', level: 'beginner', freq: 'very-common',
    q: 'What is the difference between a primary key, a foreign key and a unique key?',
    short: 'A primary key uniquely identifies each row and can’t be NULL — one per table. A unique key also blocks duplicates, and a table can have several. A foreign key references a key in another table to keep relationships valid.',
    answer: '<b>Primary key (PK):</b> uniquely identifies each row, e.g. <code>employees.id</code>. Values must be unique and <b>NOT NULL</b>, and a table has only <b>one</b> primary key — though it may span several columns (a composite key). Most databases index it automatically.<br><br><b>Unique key/constraint:</b> also prevents duplicate values, e.g. <code>employees.email</code>, and a table can have <b>many</b>. NULL handling differs: SQL Server allows only one NULL in a unique column by default, while PostgreSQL, MySQL and Oracle allow several.<br><br><b>Foreign key (FK):</b> a column that references a key in another table, e.g. <code>employees.dept_id → departments.id</code>. It enforces <b>referential integrity</b>: you can’t add an employee to a department that doesn’t exist, and deleting a department is blocked, cascaded or set to NULL depending on its <code>ON DELETE</code> rule.<br><br><b>Tester angle:</b> try to create duplicates or orphans through the UI or API and confirm the constraint (or app validation) rejects them with a sensible error; hunt for existing orphans with a LEFT JOIN … <code>IS NULL</code> query.',
    followups: ['Can a foreign key column contain NULL?', 'How would you find orphan records with SQL?'],
    mistakes: ['Saying a table can have several primary keys.', 'Claiming unique keys never allow NULL in any database.'],
    lesson: 'sa-sql-basics'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-sql-03', cat: 'sql', level: 'beginner', freq: 'very-common',
    q: 'Explain the types of SQL joins with an example.',
    short: 'INNER JOIN returns only matching rows; LEFT JOIN returns all left rows plus matches (NULLs where none); RIGHT JOIN the reverse; FULL OUTER JOIN all rows from both sides; CROSS JOIN every combination; a self join joins a table to itself.',
    answer: 'Tables: <code>employees(id, name, dept_id)</code> and <code>departments(id, name)</code>.<br><br><b>INNER JOIN</b> — only employees with a matching department:<br><code>SELECT e.name, d.name FROM employees e INNER JOIN departments d ON e.dept_id = d.id;</code><br><br><b>LEFT (OUTER) JOIN</b> — all employees; department columns are NULL where there’s no match. Handy for finding orphans:<br><code>SELECT e.name FROM employees e LEFT JOIN departments d ON e.dept_id = d.id WHERE d.id IS NULL;</code><br><br><b>RIGHT JOIN</b> — all departments, even empty ones (the same as a LEFT JOIN with the tables swapped).<br><b>FULL OUTER JOIN</b> — all rows from both sides with NULLs where unmatched. MySQL lacks it; combine a LEFT and a RIGHT JOIN with <code>UNION</code>.<br><b>CROSS JOIN</b> — every employee paired with every department (Cartesian product).<br><b>SELF JOIN</b> — a table joined to itself, e.g. employees with their managers.<br><br>Tip: if a LEFT JOIN suddenly behaves like an INNER JOIN, a <code>WHERE</code> condition on the right-hand table has probably removed the NULL rows — move it into the <code>ON</code> clause.',
    followups: ['How would you find departments with no employees?', 'What is the difference between a condition in ON and in WHERE for a LEFT JOIN?'],
    mistakes: ['Saying a LEFT JOIN returns only the non-matching rows.', 'Forgetting that unmatched columns come back as NULL.'],
    lesson: 'sa-sql-joins'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-sql-04', cat: 'sql', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'common',
    q: 'Write a query that lists each employee with their manager’s name, and one that finds employees earning more than their manager.',
    short: 'Use a self join: alias the employees table twice and join e.manager_id = m.id. A LEFT JOIN keeps employees without a manager; for the salary comparison use an inner join with WHERE e.salary > m.salary.',
    answer: 'Table: <code>employees(id, name, salary, manager_id)</code>, where <code>manager_id</code> points to another row of the same table.<br><br><b>Employee with manager name</b> — LEFT JOIN keeps the top boss, whose manager is NULL:<br><code>SELECT e.name AS employee, m.name AS manager</code><br><code>FROM employees e</code><br><code>LEFT JOIN employees m ON e.manager_id = m.id;</code><br><br><b>Employees earning more than their manager</b> — an inner join, because we need a manager to compare with:<br><code>SELECT e.name, e.salary, m.name AS manager, m.salary AS manager_salary</code><br><code>FROM employees e</code><br><code>JOIN employees m ON e.manager_id = m.id</code><br><code>WHERE e.salary &gt; m.salary;</code><br><br>The trick is the two aliases: <code>e</code> plays the employee and <code>m</code> plays the manager, even though both are the same table.<br><br>Variations interviewers like: the number of direct reports per manager (<code>GROUP BY m.id, m.name</code>), employees with no manager (<code>WHERE manager_id IS NULL</code>) and managers with nobody reporting to them (LEFT JOIN from the manager side, keeping rows where the employee side is NULL).',
    followups: ['How would you count the direct reports of each manager?', 'Why did you use a LEFT JOIN in the first query?'],
    mistakes: ['Using an inner join in the first query and silently losing the top-level manager.', 'Leaving out table aliases and getting ambiguous-column errors.'],
    lesson: 'sa-sql-joins'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-sql-05', cat: 'sql', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'very-common',
    q: 'Write a query to find the second highest salary.',
    short: '“Second highest” means the second highest distinct value. Use MAX with a subquery (salary below the maximum), DISTINCT with ORDER BY and LIMIT/OFFSET, or DENSE_RANK() — and explain how ties and a missing second value are handled.',
    answer: 'Say the salaries are 90k, 90k, 75k and 60k. The answer is <b>75k</b> — the second highest <i>distinct</i> value, not the second row.<br><br><b>1. Subquery (works almost everywhere):</b><br><code>SELECT MAX(salary) FROM employees</code><br><code>WHERE salary &lt; (SELECT MAX(salary) FROM employees);</code><br>Returns NULL when there is no second value.<br><br><b>2. DISTINCT + LIMIT/OFFSET (MySQL, PostgreSQL, SQLite):</b><br><code>SELECT DISTINCT salary FROM employees</code><br><code>ORDER BY salary DESC LIMIT 1 OFFSET 1;</code><br>Without <code>DISTINCT</code> this wrongly returns 90k. SQL Server uses <code>OFFSET 1 ROWS FETCH NEXT 1 ROWS ONLY</code>.<br><br><b>3. DENSE_RANK (also lists who earns it):</b><br><code>SELECT name, salary FROM (</code><br><code>  SELECT name, salary, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rnk</code><br><code>  FROM employees) t</code><br><code>WHERE rnk = 2;</code><br>DENSE_RANK gives ties the same rank with no gaps; <code>RANK</code> would jump to 3 after the tie, and <code>ROW_NUMBER</code> ignores ties altogether.<br><br>State your assumptions: ties count once, and NULL salaries are ignored.',
    followups: ['How would you find the second highest salary in each department?', 'What changes if two people share the top salary?'],
    mistakes: ['Using ORDER BY salary DESC LIMIT 1 OFFSET 1 without DISTINCT, which fails when the top salary is tied.', 'Using ROW_NUMBER instead of DENSE_RANK when ties exist.'],
    lesson: 'sa-sql-advanced'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-sql-06', cat: 'sql', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'common',
    q: 'How do you find the Nth highest salary?',
    short: 'Rank distinct salaries with DENSE_RANK() and keep rank N, or use DISTINCT salary ORDER BY DESC with LIMIT 1 OFFSET N−1. A correlated subquery that counts distinct higher salaries works where window functions aren’t available.',
    answer: 'Example for N = 3.<br><br><b>Window function</b> (MySQL 8+, PostgreSQL, SQL Server, Oracle):<br><code>SELECT DISTINCT salary FROM (</code><br><code>  SELECT salary, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rnk</code><br><code>  FROM employees WHERE salary IS NOT NULL) t</code><br><code>WHERE rnk = 3;</code><br><br><b>LIMIT/OFFSET</b> — the offset is N−1:<br><code>SELECT DISTINCT salary FROM employees</code><br><code>ORDER BY salary DESC LIMIT 1 OFFSET 2;</code><br><br><b>Correlated subquery</b> (portable, slower on large tables) — the Nth highest salary has exactly N−1 distinct salaries above it:<br><code>SELECT DISTINCT salary FROM employees e1</code><br><code>WHERE 2 = (SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT e2.salary) FROM employees e2</code><br><code>           WHERE e2.salary &gt; e1.salary);</code><br><br><b>Per department:</b> add <code>PARTITION BY dept_id</code> inside <code>OVER ()</code>.<br><br><b>Edge cases to mention:</b> fewer than N distinct salaries (no row or NULL — say which you expect), and NULL salaries — PostgreSQL and Oracle sort NULLs first in descending order, so filter them out as above.',
    followups: ['How would you get the third highest salary in every department?', 'What does your query return if there are only two distinct salaries?'],
    mistakes: ['Using OFFSET N instead of OFFSET N−1.', 'Forgetting DISTINCT or using ROW_NUMBER, so ties give the wrong value.'],
    lesson: 'sa-sql-advanced'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-sql-07', cat: 'sql', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'very-common',
    q: 'How do you find duplicate records in a table?',
    short: 'GROUP BY the columns that define a duplicate and keep groups with HAVING COUNT(*) > 1. To see the full duplicate rows, join that result back to the table or use COUNT(*) OVER (PARTITION BY …).',
    answer: 'First agree what “duplicate” means — the same email? The same name and date of birth? Then group by those columns:<br><code>SELECT email, COUNT(*) AS cnt</code><br><code>FROM users</code><br><code>GROUP BY email</code><br><code>HAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 1;</code><br><br><b>To see every duplicate row</b> (with ids), join back:<br><code>SELECT u.* FROM users u</code><br><code>JOIN (SELECT email FROM users GROUP BY email HAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 1) d</code><br><code>  ON u.email = d.email</code><br><code>ORDER BY u.email;</code><br>Or use a window function, <code>COUNT(*) OVER (PARTITION BY email)</code>, and keep rows where it is greater than 1.<br><br><b>Watch out for:</b> case and spaces (“Ali@x.com” vs “ali@x.com ”) — use <code>LOWER(TRIM(email))</code> if the business treats them as the same; and NULLs, which GROUP BY places in a single group.<br><br><b>Why testers care:</b> duplicates caused by a double-click on Submit, a retried API call or a data migration are classic bugs, and this read-only query proves them quickly.',
    followups: ['How would you delete the duplicates but keep one row?', 'Why can’t you write WHERE COUNT(*) > 1?'],
    mistakes: ['Using WHERE instead of HAVING to filter on COUNT.', 'Not defining which columns make two records duplicates.'],
    lesson: 'sa-sql-joins'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-sql-08', cat: 'sql', level: 'advanced', freq: 'common',
    q: 'How do you delete duplicate rows but keep one copy?',
    short: 'Keep one row per group — usually the lowest id — and delete the rest: NOT IN (SELECT MIN(id) … GROUP BY), a self-join delete, or ROW_NUMBER() > 1. Preview with SELECT first, use a transaction, then add a UNIQUE constraint.',
    answer: 'Goal: keep the row with the lowest <code>id</code> for each email and delete the others.<br><br><b>Option 1 — keep MIN(id):</b><br><code>DELETE FROM users</code><br><code>WHERE id NOT IN (SELECT MIN(id) FROM users GROUP BY email);</code><br>Works in PostgreSQL, SQL Server and Oracle. MySQL won’t let a subquery read the table being deleted from (error 1093), so there use a self join:<br><code>DELETE u1 FROM users u1 JOIN users u2</code><br><code>  ON u1.email = u2.email AND u1.id &gt; u2.id;</code><br><br><b>Option 2 — ROW_NUMBER</b> (SQL Server can delete through the CTE):<br><code>WITH r AS (SELECT id, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY email ORDER BY id) AS rn FROM users)</code><br><code>DELETE FROM r WHERE rn &gt; 1;</code><br><br><b>Safety first:</b> run the same logic as a <code>SELECT</code> to see exactly what will go; work inside a transaction and only commit when the count looks right; decide how rows with a NULL email should be treated; and do this only in test environments unless a DBA approves.<br><br>Afterwards, add a <code>UNIQUE</code> constraint so duplicates can’t return.',
    followups: ['How would you do this if the table has no id column?', 'How do you stop duplicates from coming back?'],
    mistakes: ['Running a DELETE without first previewing it with SELECT or wrapping it in a transaction.', 'Deleting every copy of a duplicated record instead of keeping one.'],
    lesson: 'sa-sql-advanced'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-sql-09', cat: 'sql', level: 'beginner', freq: 'very-common',
    q: 'What is the difference between WHERE, GROUP BY and HAVING?',
    short: 'WHERE filters individual rows before grouping; GROUP BY groups rows so aggregates (COUNT, SUM, AVG) are calculated per group; HAVING filters those groups after aggregation. Aggregates can’t be used in WHERE.',
    answer: 'Logical order of a query: <code>FROM → WHERE → GROUP BY → HAVING → SELECT → ORDER BY → LIMIT</code>.<br>• <b>WHERE</b> removes rows <i>before</i> grouping and can’t contain aggregates.<br>• <b>GROUP BY</b> collapses rows with the same value into one group per value.<br>• <b>HAVING</b> filters groups <i>after</i> aggregation, so it can use <code>COUNT</code>, <code>SUM</code> or <code>AVG</code>.<br><br>Example — departments with more than 5 active employees, with their average salary:<br><code>SELECT dept_id, COUNT(*) AS headcount, AVG(salary) AS avg_salary</code><br><code>FROM employees</code><br><code>WHERE status = \'ACTIVE\'</code><br><code>GROUP BY dept_id</code><br><code>HAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 5</code><br><code>ORDER BY headcount DESC;</code><br>WHERE drops inactive employees first, GROUP BY makes one row per department, and HAVING keeps only departments above 5.<br><br><b>Rules of thumb:</b> every non-aggregated column in SELECT belongs in GROUP BY; put plain row filters in WHERE (clearer, and indexes can help) and only aggregate conditions in HAVING.',
    followups: ['Can you use HAVING without GROUP BY?', 'Why does SELECT name, COUNT(*) FROM employees GROUP BY dept_id fail in most databases?'],
    mistakes: ['Writing WHERE COUNT(*) > 5.', 'Saying HAVING and WHERE are interchangeable.'],
    lesson: 'sa-sql-joins'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-sql-10', cat: 'sql', level: 'beginner', freq: 'very-common',
    q: 'What is the difference between DELETE, TRUNCATE and DROP?',
    short: 'DELETE removes selected rows (WHERE allowed), is logged per row and fires triggers. TRUNCATE removes all rows quickly and usually resets identity counters. DROP removes the whole table — structure, data, indexes and permissions.',
    answer: '<b>DELETE</b> (DML): <code>DELETE FROM orders WHERE status = \'TEST\';</code><br>• removes specific rows — or every row if you forget the WHERE;<br>• logged row by row and fires DELETE triggers, so it is slower on big tables;<br>• can be rolled back inside a transaction; the identity/auto-increment counter keeps counting.<br><br><b>TRUNCATE</b> (DDL): <code>TRUNCATE TABLE orders;</code><br>• removes <b>all</b> rows and takes no WHERE;<br>• much faster with minimal logging; row-level DELETE triggers don’t fire;<br>• usually resets the identity counter;<br>• may be refused when other tables reference it through foreign keys;<br>• rollback depends on the database — possible inside a transaction in SQL Server and PostgreSQL, while MySQL and Oracle commit it implicitly.<br><br><b>DROP</b> (DDL): <code>DROP TABLE orders;</code><br>• removes the table itself — data, structure, indexes, constraints and permissions. Later queries against it fail.<br><br><b>Tester angle:</b> we clean our own test data with a precise DELETE (after running the WHERE as a SELECT); TRUNCATE and DROP belong to disposable environments and need special permission.',
    followups: ['Can TRUNCATE be rolled back?', 'Which would you use to reset test data between automated runs?'],
    mistakes: ['Saying TRUNCATE accepts a WHERE clause.', 'Claiming TRUNCATE can never be rolled back in any database.'],
    lesson: 'sa-sql-basics'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-sql-11', cat: 'sql', level: 'beginner', freq: 'common',
    q: 'What is the difference between UNION and UNION ALL?',
    short: 'Both stack the results of two SELECTs with the same number of compatible columns. UNION removes duplicate rows, which costs extra work; UNION ALL keeps every row and is faster. A JOIN combines columns side by side; UNION combines rows.',
    answer: '<code>SELECT email FROM customers</code><br><code>UNION</code><br><code>SELECT email FROM newsletter_subscribers;</code><br>returns each email once, even if it appears in both tables. With <code>UNION ALL</code>, an email present in both appears twice.<br><br><b>Rules:</b> each SELECT needs the same number of columns, in the same order, with compatible types; the column names come from the first SELECT; a single <code>ORDER BY</code> at the end sorts the combined result.<br><br><b>Performance:</b> UNION must de-duplicate (sort or hash), so on large data <b>UNION ALL is faster</b>. Use it when duplicates are impossible or you actually want them — for example, counting events from two log tables.<br><br><b>UNION vs JOIN:</b> a JOIN adds columns from related tables side by side; UNION appends rows underneath each other.<br><br><b>Tester use:</b> comparing data sets — rows in the source table but missing from the migrated one with <code>EXCEPT</code> (<code>MINUS</code> in older Oracle versions) — and combining similar tables into one check query.',
    followups: ['How would you find rows that exist in one table but not in another?', 'Why is UNION usually slower than UNION ALL?'],
    mistakes: ['Confusing UNION with JOIN.', 'Saying UNION ALL removes duplicates.'],
    lesson: 'sa-sql-advanced'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-sql-12', cat: 'sql', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'common',
    q: 'What is the difference between a subquery and a join, and when would you use each?',
    short: 'A join combines columns from related tables in one result; a subquery is a query nested inside another, used for filtering or computed values. Use joins to combine data, subqueries for comparisons with aggregates and EXISTS checks.',
    answer: '<b>Join</b> — when you need columns from both tables:<br><code>SELECT e.name, d.name FROM employees e JOIN departments d ON e.dept_id = d.id;</code><br><br><b>Subquery</b> — a query inside <code>WHERE</code>, <code>FROM</code> or <code>SELECT</code>:<br><code>SELECT name FROM employees</code><br><code>WHERE salary &gt; (SELECT AVG(salary) FROM employees);</code><br><br><b>Correlated subquery</b> — refers to the outer row, so it is logically evaluated per row; e.g. employees earning above <i>their own department’s</i> average:<br><code>SELECT e.name FROM employees e</code><br><code>WHERE e.salary &gt; (SELECT AVG(x.salary) FROM employees x WHERE x.dept_id = e.dept_id);</code><br><br><b>Choosing:</b> use a join to combine data; use a subquery for comparisons with aggregates or existence checks (<code>EXISTS</code> / <code>NOT EXISTS</code>). Modern optimisers often rewrite one form into the other, so check the execution plan rather than trusting “joins are always faster”.<br><br><b>Gotcha:</b> <code>NOT IN (subquery)</code> returns no rows at all if the subquery produces a NULL; <code>NOT EXISTS</code> is the safer choice.',
    followups: ['What is a correlated subquery?', 'Why is NOT EXISTS safer than NOT IN?'],
    mistakes: ['Claiming subqueries are always slower than joins.', 'Not knowing the NOT IN with NULL trap.'],
    lesson: 'sa-sql-advanced'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-sql-13', cat: 'sql', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'common',
    q: 'What is an index, and when should or shouldn’t you add one?',
    short: 'An index is a separate structure (usually a B-tree) that lets the database find rows without scanning the whole table, like a book’s index. It speeds up filtering, joining and sorting but costs storage and slows inserts, updates and deletes.',
    answer: 'Without an index, <code>SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 42</code> may scan every row; with one on <code>customer_id</code>, the database jumps straight to the matches.<br><code>CREATE INDEX idx_orders_customer ON orders(customer_id);</code><br><br><b>Good candidates:</b> columns used often in <code>WHERE</code>, <code>JOIN … ON</code>, <code>ORDER BY</code> and <code>GROUP BY</code>; foreign keys; selective columns with many distinct values, such as email. Primary keys and unique constraints are indexed automatically in most databases.<br><br><b>Costs:</b> extra storage and slower writes, because every insert, update and delete must also update the index. Tiny tables and low-selectivity columns (a yes/no flag) rarely benefit.<br><br><b>Clustered vs non-clustered</b> (SQL Server terms): a clustered index defines the physical order of the table, so there is one per table; non-clustered indexes are separate structures pointing to rows.<br><br><b>Gotchas:</b> wrapping the column in a function (<code>WHERE YEAR(created_at) = 2025</code>) or a leading wildcard (<code>LIKE \'%son\'</code>) usually stops the index being used.<br><br><b>Tester angle:</b> slow queries found in performance tests often trace back to a missing index; <code>EXPLAIN</code> shows whether one is used.',
    followups: ['What is a composite index, and does the column order matter?', 'How would you check whether a query uses an index?'],
    mistakes: ['Saying you should index every column.', 'Not knowing that indexes slow down writes.'],
    lesson: 'sa-sql-advanced'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-sql-14', cat: 'sql', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'common',
    q: 'What is normalization? Briefly explain 1NF, 2NF and 3NF.',
    short: 'Normalization organises tables to reduce duplicated data and update anomalies. 1NF: atomic values, no repeating groups. 2NF: no column depends on only part of a composite key. 3NF: no non-key column depends on another non-key column.',
    answer: 'The goal is to store each fact once, so an update can’t leave inconsistent copies behind.<br><br><b>1NF</b> — every column holds a single (atomic) value and there are no repeating groups. A <code>phones</code> column containing “0300…, 0321…” breaks 1NF; move phone numbers into their own rows or table.<br><br><b>2NF</b> — in 1NF, and every non-key column depends on the <i>whole</i> primary key. In <code>order_items(order_id, product_id, quantity, product_name)</code>, <code>product_name</code> depends only on <code>product_id</code>, part of the key → move it to <code>products</code>.<br><br><b>3NF</b> — in 2NF, and non-key columns depend only on the key, not on other non-key columns. In <code>employees(id, dept_id, dept_name)</code>, <code>dept_name</code> depends on <code>dept_id</code> → move it to <code>departments</code>.<br><br>A handy summary: every non-key column depends on “the key, the whole key, and nothing but the key”.<br><br><b>Trade-off:</b> reporting databases are sometimes <b>denormalised</b> on purpose for faster reads.<br><b>Why testers care:</b> understanding the schema helps you write correct joins and spot anomalies, such as a renamed department still showing its old name on some screens.',
    followups: ['What is denormalization, and when is it used?', 'What is BCNF?'],
    mistakes: ['Reciting definitions without being able to give an example.', 'Claiming that more normalization is always better.'],
    lesson: 'sa-sql-basics'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-sql-15', cat: 'sql', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'occasional',
    q: 'What are ACID properties and transactions?',
    short: 'A transaction is a group of operations that succeed or fail as one unit. ACID: Atomicity (all or nothing), Consistency (rules and constraints stay valid), Isolation (concurrent transactions don’t interfere), Durability (committed data survives crashes).',
    answer: 'Moving 1,000 from account A to account B is two updates that must happen together:<br><code>BEGIN;</code><br><code>UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 1000 WHERE id = \'A\';</code><br><code>UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 1000 WHERE id = \'B\';</code><br><code>COMMIT;  -- or ROLLBACK if anything fails</code><br><br><b>Atomicity:</b> if the second update fails, the first is undone — no money vanishes.<br><b>Consistency:</b> the database moves from one valid state to another; constraints such as balance ≥ 0 and foreign keys are never broken.<br><b>Isolation:</b> concurrent transactions behave as if they ran separately, to the degree set by the isolation level (Read Committed, Repeatable Read, Serializable). Weaker levels allow dirty reads, non-repeatable reads or lost updates.<br><b>Durability:</b> once committed, the data survives a crash or power cut.<br><br><b>How testers use this:</b> simulate failures mid-operation (stop a service, break a dependency) and confirm nothing is half-saved; have two users update the same record at the same moment to look for lost updates or double spending; and wrap experiments in a transaction with ROLLBACK in test databases.',
    followups: ['What is a dirty read?', 'How would you test for a race condition such as a double withdrawal?'],
    mistakes: ['Listing the four words without explaining them through an example.', 'Confusing consistency in ACID with consistency in distributed systems (CAP theorem).'],
    lesson: 'sa-db-testing'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-sql-16', cat: 'sql', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'common',
    q: 'How does SQL handle NULL values?',
    short: 'NULL means unknown or missing — not zero or an empty string. Compare with IS NULL / IS NOT NULL (never = NULL); aggregates skip NULLs; COUNT(*) counts rows while COUNT(column) skips NULLs; COALESCE substitutes a default.',
    answer: '<b>Comparisons:</b> <code>WHERE phone = NULL</code> returns nothing, because any comparison with NULL is <i>unknown</i>. Use <code>WHERE phone IS NULL</code> or <code>IS NOT NULL</code>.<br><br><b>Aggregates:</b> <code>SUM</code>, <code>AVG</code>, <code>MIN</code> and <code>MAX</code> ignore NULLs. <code>COUNT(*)</code> counts all rows, <code>COUNT(phone)</code> only non-NULL phones. <code>AVG(bonus)</code> averages only employees who have a bonus — if NULL should mean zero, write <code>AVG(COALESCE(bonus, 0))</code>.<br><br><b>Expressions:</b> <code>salary + NULL</code> is NULL. <code>COALESCE(a, b, 0)</code> returns the first non-NULL value; <code>ISNULL</code> (SQL Server), <code>IFNULL</code> (MySQL) and <code>NVL</code> (Oracle) are dialect versions.<br><br><b>Traps:</b> <code>NOT IN (subquery)</code> with a NULL in the list returns no rows; GROUP BY puts all NULLs in one group; NULL sort order differs between databases; Oracle treats an empty string as NULL.<br><br><b>Tester angle:</b> check that optional fields are stored consistently (NULL vs empty string) and that reports and totals handle NULLs correctly — a classic source of wrong averages.',
    followups: ['What is the difference between COUNT(*) and COUNT(column)?', 'What does NULL = NULL evaluate to?'],
    mistakes: ['Writing = NULL instead of IS NULL.', 'Treating NULL as zero or as an empty string.'],
    lesson: 'sa-sql-basics'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-sql-17', cat: 'sql', level: 'beginner', freq: 'very-common',
    q: 'Write a query to count the employees in each department, including departments with no employees.',
    short: 'LEFT JOIN from departments to employees, GROUP BY the department, and use COUNT(e.id) rather than COUNT(*) so empty departments show 0 instead of 1.',
    answer: '<code>SELECT d.name AS department, COUNT(e.id) AS employee_count</code><br><code>FROM departments d</code><br><code>LEFT JOIN employees e ON e.dept_id = d.id</code><br><code>GROUP BY d.id, d.name</code><br><code>ORDER BY employee_count DESC;</code><br><br><b>Why each choice matters:</b><br>• <b>LEFT JOIN from departments</b> keeps departments with no employees; an INNER JOIN would drop them.<br>• <b>COUNT(e.id)</b> counts only matched employees, so an empty department shows <b>0</b>. <code>COUNT(*)</code> would show 1, because the LEFT JOIN still produces one row (full of NULLs) for that department.<br>• <b>GROUP BY d.id, d.name</b> — grouping by id avoids merging two departments that happen to share a name.<br><br>If only departments that have employees matter, the simple version is enough:<br><code>SELECT dept_id, COUNT(*) FROM employees GROUP BY dept_id;</code><br><br>Typical follow-ups: departments with more than 10 employees (<code>HAVING COUNT(e.id) &gt; 10</code>), the department with the highest headcount, or the average salary per department.',
    followups: ['How would you show only departments with more than 10 employees?', 'Which department has the most employees?'],
    mistakes: ['Using COUNT(*) with a LEFT JOIN, which shows 1 instead of 0 for empty departments.', 'Using an INNER JOIN and losing departments without employees.'],
    lesson: 'sa-sql-joins'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-sql-18', cat: 'sql', level: 'beginner', freq: 'very-common',
    q: 'Why does a tester need SQL, and how would you verify data after an API call or UI action?',
    short: 'Because the UI or API can report success while the stored data is wrong. Testers use read-only SQL to verify persistence and side effects, find or prepare test data, check integrity (orphans, duplicates) and back up bug reports with evidence.',
    answer: '<b>Uses of SQL in testing:</b><br>• <b>Verify persistence</b> — a 201 or a “Saved!” message doesn’t prove the data was stored correctly.<br>• <b>Check side effects</b> — stock reduced, audit row written, status changed, nothing else touched.<br>• <b>Find or prepare test data</b> — a user with an expired subscription, an order in a particular state.<br>• <b>Data integrity</b> — orphans, duplicates, unexpected NULLs; migration checks with row counts and totals.<br>• <b>Debugging</b> — attach the query and its result to the bug report.<br><br><b>Example:</b> <code>POST /orders</code> returns 201 with id 981. Then:<br><code>SELECT status, total, customer_id FROM orders WHERE id = 981;</code><br><code>SELECT product_id, quantity FROM order_items WHERE order_id = 981;</code><br><code>SELECT stock FROM products WHERE id = 55;  -- compare with the value before</code><br>Compare each value with the request and business rules (total = items + tax − discount, status = \'PENDING\').<br><br><b>Good habits:</b> use a read-only account on shared environments, never UPDATE or DELETE data you didn’t create, and stay out of production databases unless you are explicitly allowed.',
    followups: ['The API reports success but the row is missing — what do you do?', 'How would you verify data when you have no database access?'],
    mistakes: ['Treating the API status or a UI message as proof that data was saved.', 'Running UPDATE or DELETE on shared environments without permission.'],
    lesson: 'sa-db-testing'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-sql-19', cat: 'sql', level: 'advanced', freq: 'occasional',
    q: 'How would you test a stored procedure or a trigger?',
    short: 'Treat a stored procedure like a function: call it with valid, boundary, NULL and invalid inputs; check outputs, changed rows, errors and rollback. For a trigger, run the change that fires it, verify the effect, and check when it shouldn’t fire.',
    answer: '<b>Stored procedure</b> — named SQL logic stored in the database, run with <code>CALL apply_monthly_interest(\'2025-06\');</code> (<code>EXEC</code> in SQL Server).<br>Tests:<br>• valid inputs → correct result set or output parameters, and exactly the expected rows changed;<br>• boundary and NULL inputs, wrong types, unknown ids → a clear error and no partial changes (the transaction rolls back);<br>• running it twice — safe to repeat, or does it apply interest twice?<br>• permissions — only the right roles can execute it;<br>• run time on realistic data volumes.<br><br><b>Trigger</b> — code that runs automatically on INSERT, UPDATE or DELETE, such as writing an audit row when a salary changes.<br>Tests:<br>• perform the action and verify the effect (audit row with old and new values, user and timestamp);<br>• cases where it must <i>not</i> fire (an update to an unrelated column);<br>• bulk operations — does it handle 1,000 rows or only the first?<br>• an error inside the trigger rolls back the original change.<br><br>Always start from known data, compare results with queries, and clean up — ideally in a disposable test database.',
    followups: ['How would you test a trigger that writes to an audit table?', 'How do you make database tests repeatable?'],
    mistakes: ['Testing only the happy path with a single input.', 'Forgetting to check that a failure leaves no partial changes.'],
    lesson: 'sa-db-testing'
  },

  /* ===================== Mobile testing ===================== */
  {
    id: 'iq-mob-01', cat: 'mobile', level: 'beginner', freq: 'very-common',
    q: 'What is the difference between native, hybrid and mobile web apps?',
    short: 'Native apps are built for one platform (Kotlin/Java for Android, Swift for iOS) and installed from a store. Hybrid apps wrap web code in a native shell with a WebView. Mobile web apps run in the phone’s browser with no installation.',
    answer: '<b>Native:</b> written with each platform’s own languages and SDKs — Kotlin/Java on Android, Swift/Objective-C on iOS. Best performance and full access to device features (camera, GPS, biometrics, background services), but separate codebases. Installed from Google Play or the App Store.<br><br><b>Hybrid:</b> HTML/CSS/JavaScript running inside a native container through a WebView (e.g. Ionic with Capacitor or Cordova). One codebase, store distribution, device features through plugins; performance and look-and-feel can lag behind native. Cross-platform frameworks such as Flutter and React Native are often discussed alongside, although they render native or custom UI rather than a WebView.<br><br><b>Mobile web:</b> a responsive website in the mobile browser. PWAs add offline caching, home-screen installation and push notifications, with platform limits.<br><br><b>Testing impact:</b><br>• native and hybrid: installs, upgrades, permissions, interruptions, store builds, device fragmentation; Appium with native and WebView contexts;<br>• hybrid: WebView rendering and the bridge between web and native code;<br>• mobile web: browsers, screen sizes, responsive layout and network — closer to web testing, plus real-device checks.',
    followups: ['How would your test approach differ for a hybrid app?', 'What is a PWA, and what can’t it do that a native app can?'],
    mistakes: ['Saying hybrid apps need no device testing because they are “just web”.', 'Calling a responsive website a native app.'],
    lesson: 'wm-mobile-basics'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-mob-02', cat: 'mobile', level: 'beginner', freq: 'very-common',
    q: 'What is the difference between an emulator, a simulator and a real device? When do you use each?',
    short: 'An emulator mimics device hardware and runs the real OS (Android Emulator); a simulator mimics only the software (iOS Simulator). Both suit early and automated testing but don’t replace real devices for performance, sensors, battery and OEM quirks.',
    answer: '<b>Emulator:</b> recreates the device’s hardware and runs a real OS image — e.g. the Android Emulator in Android Studio. Good OS fidelity, but it runs on a computer’s CPU and memory.<br><b>Simulator:</b> imitates the software environment without emulating hardware — e.g. Xcode’s iOS Simulator, which runs apps built for the Mac. Fast, but further from a real iPhone.<br><b>Real device:</b> actual phones, owned by the team or rented in a device cloud.<br><br><b>Emulators and simulators suit:</b> early checks during development, many OS versions and screen sizes at low cost, layout checks, automation in CI, debugging with logs.<br><br><b>Real devices are needed for:</b> performance and memory, battery and heat, camera, GPS, fingerprint and face unlock, Bluetooth/NFC, real network conditions, calls and SMS interruptions, real push delivery, manufacturer customisations (such as aggressive battery savers killing background apps), gestures and the final release sign-off.<br><br>A balanced setup: most automated runs on emulators and simulators, plus a prioritised set of real devices — physical or in a device cloud — chosen from user analytics.',
    followups: ['A test passes on the emulator but fails on a real device — what do you check?', 'What is a device cloud, and when is it worth paying for?'],
    mistakes: ['Saying emulators are enough, so real devices aren’t needed.', 'Using “emulator” and “simulator” as if they meant exactly the same thing.'],
    lesson: 'wm-devices'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-mob-03', cat: 'mobile', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'very-common',
    q: 'What is interruption testing in mobile apps? Give examples.',
    short: 'It checks that the app copes with events that pause or disturb it — calls, SMS, notifications, alarms, low battery, network loss, app switching, screen lock, charger or headphones — without crashing, losing data or corrupting state.',
    answer: 'A phone is never running only our app, so interruption testing asks: what happens when something else takes over mid-task?<br><br><b>Common interruptions:</b><br>• incoming call (answered or rejected), SMS, another app’s notification;<br>• alarm or timer;<br>• low-battery warning, battery saver switching on, device shutting down;<br>• network loss, Wi-Fi ↔ mobile data switch, airplane mode;<br>• screen lock, Home button, switching apps and coming back;<br>• plugging in a charger or headphones, a Bluetooth device connecting;<br>• screen rotation, OS update prompts, permission dialogs.<br><br><b>What to verify:</b> no crash or freeze; the app resumes on the same screen with data intact (a half-filled form, video position, game state); in-progress operations such as payments or uploads complete, retry or fail clearly without duplicates; audio pauses and resumes properly.<br><br><b>High-value example:</b> a call arrives during the OTP step of a PayLite payment — afterwards the user can finish or retry safely and is never charged twice.<br><br>Most of this needs real devices; some steps can be scripted with ADB or Appium commands.',
    followups: ['How would you test a call arriving during a video upload?', 'Which interruptions can be automated, and how?'],
    mistakes: ['Naming only “incoming call” as an interruption.', 'Checking only that the app doesn’t crash, not that state and data are preserved.'],
    lesson: 'wm-mobile-conditions'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-mob-04', cat: 'mobile', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'common',
    q: 'How do you test a mobile app under different network conditions?',
    short: 'Test Wi-Fi, 4G/5G, slow and high-latency links, packet loss, offline and network switching — checking loading states, timeouts, retries without duplicates, offline caching and sync, and clear errors — with throttling tools plus real-world checks.',
    answer: 'Users’ connections vary hugely, so I test far more than office Wi-Fi.<br><br><b>Conditions:</b> fast Wi-Fi, 4G/5G, slow 3G/2G-like speeds, high latency, packet loss, fully offline, airplane mode on and off, Wi-Fi ↔ mobile data handover, captive portals (hotel Wi-Fi login pages), VPN.<br><br><b>What to check:</b><br>• loading indicators instead of frozen screens, and sensible timeouts;<br>• retries that don’t duplicate actions (a payment or message sent twice);<br>• offline mode — cached content readable, actions queued and synced later, conflicts handled;<br>• friendly error messages with a retry option;<br>• images and video adapt to bandwidth; large downloads resume;<br>• reasonable data usage on mobile data.<br><br><b>Tools:</b> Android Emulator network settings, Apple’s Network Link Conditioner, proxies such as Charles or mitmproxy for throttling and failure injection, DevTools throttling for mobile web, and device clouds with network profiles. Finish with some real-world testing on actual mobile networks.<br><br>Prioritise flows that send data — payments, form submissions, uploads — because that is where poor networks cause duplicates and data loss.',
    followups: ['How would you test offline sync conflicts?', 'How can you simulate packet loss?'],
    mistakes: ['Testing only on fast office Wi-Fi.', 'Checking only the “no internet” message and never slow or unstable connections.'],
    lesson: 'wm-mobile-conditions'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-mob-05', cat: 'mobile', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'common',
    q: 'How do you test app permissions on Android and iOS?',
    short: 'Test each runtime permission (camera, location, contacts, notifications…) when allowed, denied, permanently denied, allowed only once or approximately, and revoked later in Settings — the app should explain why, degrade gracefully and never crash.',
    answer: 'Android (6.0 and later) and iOS ask for sensitive permissions at runtime, and users can change them at any time.<br><br><b>Scenarios per permission:</b><br>• <b>Allow</b> → the feature works.<br>• <b>Deny</b> → the feature is disabled with a clear explanation; the rest of the app still works.<br>• <b>Denied permanently</b> → the system prompt can’t appear again, so the app should guide the user to Settings.<br>• <b>Only this time</b> and <b>while using the app</b> vs background access.<br>• <b>Approximate vs precise location</b>; iOS <b>limited photo access</b>.<br>• <b>Revoked in Settings</b> while the app is in the background → no crash on return (Android restarts the app’s process).<br>• <b>Notifications</b> — explicit consent on iOS and on Android 13+.<br><br><b>Also check:</b> permission requested at the moment of need with a clear reason, not all at first launch; no requests for permissions the app doesn’t need (privacy); permission state survives upgrades; behaviour across OS versions, because the rules keep changing.<br><br>To cover states quickly in automation, use <code>adb shell pm grant</code> / <code>pm revoke</code> or Appium capabilities.',
    followups: ['What should happen if a user permanently denies camera access?', 'How would you automate different permission states?'],
    mistakes: ['Testing only the “Allow” path.', 'Forgetting that permissions can be revoked later from Settings.'],
    lesson: 'wm-mobile-conditions'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-mob-06', cat: 'mobile', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'common',
    q: 'How do you test app lifecycle and background behaviour?',
    short: 'Move the app through its states — launch, background, foreground, killed by the user or the OS, relaunch — and check that state and data are restored, sessions and timers behave, background work runs as designed, and nothing crashes or leaks.',
    answer: 'Mobile operating systems pause, stop and kill apps to save memory and battery, so the app must survive every transition.<br><br><b>Scenarios:</b><br>• <b>Background → foreground</b> after seconds, minutes and hours: same screen, form data kept, session still valid or expired correctly, data refreshed.<br>• <b>OS kills the app in the background</b> (low memory): on return it restores state or restarts cleanly. On Android, Developer options → “Don’t keep activities” forces this.<br>• <b>User force-closes</b> it from the app switcher: the next launch is clean, with no corrupted data.<br>• <b>Configuration changes</b> — rotation, language, dark mode, font size — without losing input.<br>• <b>Background work:</b> music keeps playing, uploads continue or resume, location tracking respects the permission, notifications open the right screen even from a killed state.<br>• <b>Cold vs warm start</b> times.<br>• <b>Device restart:</b> scheduled reminders still fire.<br><br><b>Watch for:</b> crashes on resume, duplicate requests, memory growing with each switch, battery drain from background tasks, and manufacturer battery savers killing background work. Logcat and the Xcode console help spot lifecycle errors.',
    followups: ['What is the difference between a cold start and a warm start?', 'How would you test that a long upload continues in the background?'],
    mistakes: ['Only ever testing with the app in the foreground.', 'Assuming state restoration that works on one device works on every manufacturer’s phone.'],
    lesson: 'wm-mobile-conditions'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-mob-07', cat: 'mobile', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'common',
    q: 'What do you check in mobile app installation, upgrade and uninstall testing?',
    short: 'Fresh installs (including low storage and interruptions), upgrades from several older versions that keep user data and login, data migrations, forced-update prompts, old app versions against the new backend, and clean uninstall/reinstall behaviour.',
    answer: '<b>Installation:</b> from the store, TestFlight or an internal build; first launch, onboarding and permission prompts; low storage (clear error); interrupted download; minimum supported OS version enforced; app name, icon, size and splash screen correct.<br><br><b>Upgrade (the riskiest part):</b><br>• update from the previous version <i>and</i> from older versions still in use — not just the last one;<br>• the user stays logged in; settings, drafts, offline data and downloads survive;<br>• local database migrations run correctly;<br>• new permissions are requested properly; notifications still work;<br>• forced-update and “update available” prompts behave as designed;<br>• old app versions still work against the updated backend (API compatibility).<br><br><b>Uninstall and reinstall:</b> app data is removed from the device unless backed up by design; reinstalling gives a clean state or restores from cloud backup as intended; check Android auto-backup, and on iOS remember that Keychain items can survive an uninstall, so stale login data may reappear.<br><br>If possible, automate upgrade paths in CI: install the old build, create data, install the new build over it, verify.',
    followups: ['Why test upgrades from versions older than the previous release?', 'How does a forced update work, and how would you test it?'],
    mistakes: ['Testing only fresh installs of the new build.', 'Forgetting that old app versions keep calling the updated backend.'],
    lesson: 'wm-mobile-basics'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-mob-08', cat: 'mobile', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'common',
    q: 'How do you choose which devices to test on, given device fragmentation?',
    short: 'Use data: analytics or market share for your users’ devices, OS versions, screen sizes and manufacturers. Build a tiered device matrix with popular, low-end and latest devices, and extend coverage with emulators and device clouds.',
    answer: 'There are thousands of Android models with different OS versions, screen sizes, chipsets and manufacturer skins, plus several iOS versions in use — testing them all is impossible.<br><br><b>Selection approach:</b><br>1. <b>Data first:</b> app analytics and crash reports show which devices and OS versions <i>our</i> users have. For a new app, use market share in the target countries — in Pakistan and India, budget and mid-range Android phones dominate.<br>2. <b>Cover dimensions, not just models:</b> OS versions (latest, oldest supported, most common), screen sizes and densities, RAM (include a low-end device), several manufacturers (each customises Android differently), notches and foldables if relevant.<br>3. <b>Tier it:</b> Tier 1 — top devices, full regression; Tier 2 — smoke tests and key flows; Tier 3 — occasional checks in a device cloud.<br>4. <b>Mix tools:</b> emulators and simulators for breadth, a small in-house set of real phones, and a device cloud for the long tail.<br>5. <b>Review regularly</b> as new devices and OS versions appear and old ones fade.',
    followups: ['Why include a low-end device in the matrix?', 'How often would you update the device list?'],
    mistakes: ['Testing only on the newest flagship phones the team happens to own.', 'Picking devices at random instead of from user data.'],
    lesson: 'wm-devices'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-mob-09', cat: 'mobile', level: 'beginner', freq: 'very-common',
    q: 'How is mobile app testing different from web testing, and what kinds of bugs are mobile-specific?',
    short: 'Mobile adds fragmentation, gestures, interruptions, unstable networks, permissions, battery and memory limits, app lifecycle, installs and updates. Typical bugs: crashes on resume, data lost on rotation, keyboard hiding fields, broken small-screen layouts.',
    answer: '<b>What mobile adds:</b><br>• many devices, OS versions and screen sizes;<br>• touch gestures (tap, swipe, pinch, long-press) and on-screen keyboards;<br>• interruptions (calls, notifications) and the app lifecycle (background, killed);<br>• changing networks and offline use;<br>• runtime permissions and sensors (GPS, camera, biometrics);<br>• limited battery, memory and storage;<br>• installs, upgrades, store review — and old app versions that stay in use.<br><br><b>Typical mobile-specific bugs:</b><br>• crash or blank screen when returning from the background;<br>• form data lost after rotation;<br>• the keyboard covering an input or the submit button;<br>• layouts breaking on small screens, notches, large font settings or foldables;<br>• hangs on slow networks, duplicate submissions after reconnecting;<br>• a notification opening the wrong screen;<br>• battery drain or overheating from background work;<br>• works on one manufacturer but not another;<br>• unexpected Android Back-button behaviour.<br><br>Web testing focuses more on browsers, window sizes and URLs; mobile testing means thinking about the device and how people use it on the move.',
    followups: ['Give an example of a mobile bug you found and how you reported it.', 'What extra details belong in a mobile bug report?'],
    mistakes: ['Saying mobile testing is just web testing on a smaller screen.', 'Leaving device model, OS version and app build out of mobile bug reports.'],
    lesson: 'wm-mobile-basics'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-mob-10', cat: 'mobile', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'common',
    q: 'What is Appium, and how does it work?',
    short: 'Appium is an open-source tool that automates native, hybrid and mobile web apps on Android and iOS via the WebDriver protocol. Client libraries send commands to the Appium server, whose drivers (UiAutomator2 for Android, XCUITest for iOS) control the device.',
    answer: '<b>Architecture:</b> the test (Java, Python, JavaScript, C#…) uses an Appium client library → sends WebDriver commands over HTTP to the <b>Appium server</b> → the server hands them to a <b>driver</b>: <b>UiAutomator2</b> (or Espresso) for Android, <b>XCUITest</b> for iOS → the driver controls a real device, emulator or simulator. In Appium 2, drivers are installed separately, e.g. <code>appium driver install uiautomator2</code>.<br><br><b>Capabilities</b> describe the session: <code>platformName</code>, <code>appium:automationName</code>, <code>appium:deviceName</code>, <code>appium:app</code> (path to the APK or IPA), or an app package and activity.<br><br><b>Locators:</b> prefer <b>accessibility id</b> (content-desc on Android, accessibilityIdentifier on iOS) or resource-id; XPath works but is slow and brittle. <b>Appium Inspector</b> helps find elements.<br><br><b>Hybrid apps:</b> switch between the <code>NATIVE_APP</code> and <code>WEBVIEW</code> contexts.<br><br><b>Strengths:</b> no changes to the app code, one API for both platforms, many languages, works with device clouds.<br><b>Challenges:</b> slower than Espresso or XCUITest, setup effort, flakiness from animations and timing — use explicit waits and stable ids.',
    followups: ['Why is accessibility id preferred over XPath?', 'How do you automate a WebView inside a hybrid app?'],
    mistakes: ['Saying Appium works only for Android, or only for native apps.', 'Relying mostly on long absolute XPath locators.'],
    lesson: 'wm-devices'
  },

  /* ===================== Non-functional: performance, security, accessibility, usability, compatibility, localisation ===================== */
  {
    id: 'iq-nfr-01', cat: 'nonfunctional', level: 'beginner', freq: 'very-common',
    q: 'What is the difference between load, stress, spike, soak and volume testing?',
    short: 'Load: expected normal and peak traffic. Stress: beyond capacity, to find the breaking point and how it fails and recovers. Spike: sudden jumps in users. Soak (endurance): steady load for hours to expose leaks. Volume: very large amounts of data.',
    answer: 'Example: a ticket-booking site expecting 2,000 concurrent users at peak.<br><br><b>Load testing:</b> apply the expected load (normal and peak, e.g. 2,000 users) and compare response times, throughput and error rate with the targets — “does it meet the agreed limits under realistic conditions?”<br><b>Stress testing:</b> keep increasing beyond the expected load (3,000, 5,000…) to find the breaking point, see <i>how</i> it fails (graceful errors or crashes and corrupted data) and check that it recovers when the load drops.<br><b>Spike testing:</b> jump suddenly from 200 to 4,000 users — tickets going on sale, a flash sale — and check autoscaling, queues and recovery.<br><b>Soak / endurance testing:</b> hold a realistic load for many hours (e.g. 8–24) to reveal memory leaks, exhausted connection pools, growing logs or gradual slowdown.<br><b>Volume testing:</b> use large data volumes — millions of rows, huge files, long histories — to see how queries, reports and backups behave.<br><br>Related terms: <b>scalability testing</b> (how performance changes as users or resources grow) and <b>capacity testing</b> (the maximum load that still meets targets). All of these are types of <b>performance testing</b>.',
    followups: ['Which type of test would find a memory leak?', 'How would you decide the target load for a load test?'],
    mistakes: ['Using load testing and stress testing as synonyms.', 'Thinking performance testing only means measuring page load time.'],
    lesson: 'qb-performance'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-nfr-02', cat: 'nonfunctional', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'common',
    q: 'Which metrics do you look at in performance testing?',
    short: 'Response time (average plus percentiles such as p90, p95, p99), throughput (requests or transactions per second), error rate, concurrent users and server resources (CPU, memory, DB connections). Percentiles matter more than averages, which hide slow outliers.',
    answer: '<b>User-facing:</b><br>• <b>Response time / latency</b> — time from request to complete response. Report <b>percentiles</b>: p95 = 2 s means 95% of requests finished within 2 seconds. Averages hide outliers — an average of 1 s can include 5% of users waiting 10 s.<br>• <b>Throughput</b> — requests or business transactions per second or minute.<br>• <b>Error rate</b> — percentage of failed requests (5xx, timeouts, wrong content). A fast error page is not a success.<br>• <b>Concurrent / virtual users</b> — the load being applied.<br><br><b>System-side:</b> CPU, memory, disk and network I/O, thread and connection pools, database query times, garbage collection, queue lengths.<br><br><b>Web front end:</b> Core Web Vitals — LCP (loading), INP (responsiveness), CLS (visual stability).<br><br><b>Reading them together:</b> as load rises, throughput should rise and then level off; if response times climb sharply while throughput stays flat, a bottleneck has been reached.<br><br>Agree targets up front, for example “p95 under 800 ms at 1,000 users with an error rate below 1%”.',
    followups: ['Why is p95 more useful than the average?', 'What is the difference between latency and throughput?'],
    mistakes: ['Reporting only the average response time.', 'Ignoring the error rate, so a fast run full of errors looks like a success.'],
    lesson: 'qb-perf-metrics'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-nfr-03', cat: 'nonfunctional', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'common',
    q: 'Explain the basic components of a JMeter test plan.',
    short: 'A Test Plan holds Thread Groups (virtual users, ramp-up, loops or duration), Samplers (e.g. HTTP Request), Config Elements (CSV Data Set, Header Manager), Timers (think time), Assertions, Pre/Post-Processors (extract values) and Listeners (results).',
    answer: 'Apache JMeter is an open-source, Java-based load-testing tool.<br>• <b>Test Plan</b> — the root container.<br>• <b>Thread Group</b> — the virtual users: number of threads, <b>ramp-up period</b> (100 threads over 50 s starts about two users per second), loop count or duration.<br>• <b>Samplers</b> — the requests sent, mostly <b>HTTP Request</b> (also JDBC, JMS and others).<br>• <b>Config Elements</b> — HTTP Request Defaults, <b>HTTP Header Manager</b>, Cookie Manager, <b>CSV Data Set Config</b> for test data such as many user accounts.<br>• <b>Timers</b> — think time between requests (Constant, Uniform Random) so users behave realistically.<br>• <b>Assertions</b> — Response, JSON and Duration assertions mark a sample as failed when content or timing is wrong.<br>• <b>Pre/Post-Processors</b> — e.g. <b>JSON Extractor</b> or Regular Expression Extractor to capture a token and reuse it (correlation).<br>• <b>Listeners</b> — View Results Tree for debugging; Summary and Aggregate Reports for averages, percentiles, throughput and error %.<br><br><b>Good practice:</b> build the plan in the GUI but run real tests in non-GUI mode — <code>jmeter -n -t plan.jmx -l results.jtl -e -o report</code> — with heavy listeners disabled.',
    followups: ['What is correlation in JMeter, and why is it needed?', 'Why should load tests run in non-GUI mode?'],
    mistakes: ['Running large tests in GUI mode with View Results Tree enabled.', 'Leaving out timers, so virtual users fire requests unrealistically fast.'],
    lesson: 'qb-performance'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-nfr-04', cat: 'nonfunctional', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'occasional',
    q: 'What is k6, and what does a simple load test look like?',
    short: 'k6 is an open-source load-testing tool from Grafana Labs. Tests are JavaScript files run from the command line; you define virtual users and stages, checks for correctness, and thresholds that fail the run when targets such as p95 response time are missed.',
    answer: 'k6 runs test scripts written in JavaScript on an engine written in Go, so it is light on resources and fits neatly into CI pipelines.<br><br><code>import http from \'k6/http\';</code><br><code>import { check, sleep } from \'k6\';</code><br><code>export const options = {</code><br><code>  stages: [{ duration: \'1m\', target: 50 }, { duration: \'3m\', target: 50 }, { duration: \'1m\', target: 0 }],</code><br><code>  thresholds: { http_req_duration: [\'p(95)&lt;800\'], http_req_failed: [\'rate&lt;0.01\'] },</code><br><code>};</code><br><code>export default function () {</code><br><code>  const res = http.get(\'https://staging.shopflow.test/api/products\');</code><br><code>  check(res, { \'status is 200\': (r) => r.status === 200 });</code><br><code>  sleep(1);</code><br><code>}</code><br><br><b>VUs</b> (virtual users) run the default function in a loop; <b>stages</b> ramp the load up and down; <b>checks</b> verify responses but don’t fail the run on their own; <b>thresholds</b> are the pass/fail criteria — if p95 exceeds 800 ms or more than 1% of requests fail, k6 exits with a non-zero code and the CI job fails.<br><br>Run it with <code>k6 run script.js</code>. Compared with JMeter: code instead of a GUI and easy version control.',
    followups: ['What is the difference between a check and a threshold in k6?', 'How would you run k6 in a CI pipeline?'],
    mistakes: ['Assuming failed checks automatically fail the whole run.', 'Running a load test against a shared or production environment without agreement.'],
    lesson: 'qb-performance'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-nfr-05', cat: 'nonfunctional', level: 'advanced', freq: 'common',
    q: 'How do you find a performance bottleneck?',
    short: 'Reproduce the slowdown with a controlled load test, correlate response times with server metrics (CPU, memory, database, threads, network), then narrow it down with APM traces, slow-query logs and profiling — changing one thing at a time.',
    answer: '<b>1. Define and reproduce:</b> which transaction is slow, and at what load? Ramp up gradually and note where response times start climbing while throughput stops growing — the “knee” of the curve.<br><br><b>2. Correlate with resource metrics</b> on each tier (load balancer, app servers, database, caches, external APIs):<br>• CPU near 100% → inefficient code or too few instances;<br>• memory steadily rising → a leak or long garbage-collection pauses;<br>• thread or connection pool exhausted → requests queue while CPU looks idle;<br>• slow queries → missing indexes, N+1 queries, locks;<br>• network or third-party latency.<br><br><b>3. Drill down:</b> APM tools and distributed tracing show where each request spends its time; database slow-query logs and <code>EXPLAIN</code>; profilers; logs with timings.<br><br><b>4. Isolate:</b> test endpoints individually, mock external services, compare with a baseline build.<br><br><b>5. Fix and re-test:</b> change one thing at a time and rerun the same test.<br><br>Also rule out the test itself — an overloaded load generator, missing think time or unrealistic data can create false bottlenecks. My job is clear evidence (graphs, timings) so developers can pinpoint the cause.',
    followups: ['What does it mean if CPU is low but response times are high?', 'How can the load generator itself become the bottleneck?'],
    mistakes: ['Blaming “the server” without metrics showing which layer is slow.', 'Changing several settings at once, so nobody knows what helped.'],
    lesson: 'qb-performance'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-nfr-06', cat: 'nonfunctional', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'very-common',
    q: 'What is security testing, and what is the OWASP Top 10?',
    short: 'Security testing checks that data and functions are protected from misuse: authentication, authorization, input handling, data protection, configuration. The OWASP Top 10 is a widely used list of the most critical web app security risks (latest edition: 2025).',
    answer: '<b>Security testing</b> looks for weaknesses that let someone see, change or break what they shouldn’t. Areas a QA engineer typically covers: authentication (password rules, lockout, MFA, session timeout), authorization (role checks, access to other users’ data), input handling (injection, XSS), sensitive data (HTTPS, no secrets in logs or URLs), error handling (no stack traces), security headers and cookie flags.<br><br><b>OWASP Top 10, 2025 edition:</b> A01 Broken Access Control · A02 Security Misconfiguration · A03 Software Supply Chain Failures · A04 Cryptographic Failures · A05 Injection · A06 Insecure Design · A07 Authentication Failures · A08 Software or Data Integrity Failures · A09 Security Logging and Alerting Failures · A10 Mishandling of Exceptional Conditions. (Many interviewers still refer to the 2021 list, which also had broken access control at number one.)<br><br>You don’t need to recite it perfectly; naming broken access control and giving a test — user A opening user B’s invoice by changing the id — is more convincing.<br><br><b>Tools:</b> DevTools, OWASP ZAP or Burp Suite, dependency scanners. Only test systems you’re explicitly authorized to test; scans complement, not replace, specialist penetration testing.',
    followups: ['How would you test for broken access control?', 'What is the difference between vulnerability scanning and penetration testing?'],
    mistakes: ['Saying security is only the security team’s job.', 'Offering to run attack tools against any website to “check” it.'],
    lesson: 'qb-security'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-nfr-07', cat: 'nonfunctional', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'common',
    q: 'What are SQL injection and XSS, and how would a tester check for them safely?',
    short: 'SQL injection is input that changes a database query; XSS is input that runs as script in another user’s browser. Testers check, only on systems they’re authorized to test, that harmless probes are rejected or treated as plain text, with no errors or leaks.',
    answer: '<b>SQL injection:</b> the app builds SQL by gluing user input into a query, so input such as <code>\' OR \'1\'=\'1</code> can change the query’s logic — bypassing a login or exposing other data. <b>Fix:</b> parameterised queries (prepared statements), input validation, least-privilege database accounts.<br><br><b>Cross-site scripting (XSS):</b> the app writes user input into a page without encoding it, so a value like <code>&lt;script&gt;alert(1)&lt;/script&gt;</code> runs in other users’ browsers and can steal sessions or alter the page. Types: <b>stored</b> (saved in a comment or profile), <b>reflected</b> (echoed from a URL or parameter) and <b>DOM-based</b>. <b>Fix:</b> context-aware output encoding, input validation, a Content-Security-Policy, HttpOnly cookies.<br><br><b>Checking safely:</b><br>• get written authorization and use a test environment — never production or someone else’s site;<br>• use harmless probes (a single quote, <code>alert(1)</code>), never destructive payloads;<br>• look for SQL errors, stack traces, changed behaviour, or script running instead of showing as text;<br>• check stored values display encoded everywhere — lists, emails, admin screens;<br>• use an approved scanner such as OWASP ZAP and report findings privately to the team.',
    followups: ['What is the difference between stored and reflected XSS?', 'Why do prepared statements prevent SQL injection?'],
    mistakes: ['Trying payloads on live or third-party sites without permission.', 'Believing client-side validation alone prevents injection.'],
    lesson: 'qb-security'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-nfr-08', cat: 'nonfunctional', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'common',
    q: 'What is accessibility testing, and what is WCAG?',
    short: 'Accessibility testing checks that people with disabilities — visual, hearing, motor, cognitive — can use the product, often with assistive technology. WCAG (now 2.2) is the W3C standard, built on POUR, with levels A, AA and AAA; AA is the usual target.',
    answer: '<b>WCAG</b> (Web Content Accessibility Guidelines) from the W3C is the reference standard; <b>2.2</b> is the current version. It rests on four principles — <b>POUR</b>: Perceivable, Operable, Understandable, Robust — with testable success criteria at levels <b>A</b> (minimum), <b>AA</b> (what most laws and contracts require) and <b>AAA</b> (enhanced).<br><br><b>Manual checks I always do:</b><br>• <b>Keyboard only:</b> every control reachable with Tab, logical order, visible focus, no keyboard traps, Enter/Space activate controls.<br>• <b>Screen reader</b> (NVDA, VoiceOver, TalkBack): meaningful labels, headings and link text; errors announced.<br>• <b>Images:</b> useful alt text; decorative images ignored.<br>• <b>Colour:</b> contrast of at least 4.5:1 for normal text at AA; information never conveyed by colour alone.<br>• <b>Forms:</b> labels tied to inputs, clear error messages with suggestions.<br>• <b>Zoom and reflow:</b> 200% text and narrow screens without losing content; captions for video.<br><br><b>Automated tools</b> (axe, Lighthouse, WAVE) catch only part of the issues — great in CI, but manual and assistive-technology testing is still required.',
    followups: ['How would you test a form for accessibility?', 'Why can’t automated tools find all accessibility issues?'],
    mistakes: ['Relying only on an automated scanner score.', 'Thinking accessibility is only about blind users.'],
    lesson: 'qb-accessibility'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-nfr-09', cat: 'nonfunctional', level: 'beginner', freq: 'occasional',
    q: 'What is usability testing, and how is it done?',
    short: 'Usability testing watches representative users try realistic tasks, to see how easily, quickly and happily they succeed. You measure task success, time, errors and satisfaction, then fix the biggest friction points. A bug-free feature can still fail usability.',
    answer: 'Usability asks: can the intended users reach their goals <b>effectively, efficiently and with satisfaction</b>? A feature can pass every functional test and still confuse people.<br><br><b>How it’s done:</b><br>1. Define goals and target users — e.g. first-time patients booking on MediBook.<br>2. Write realistic tasks: “Book a skin-specialist appointment for next Tuesday.”<br>3. Recruit a handful of representative users per round; around five often reveal most major problems.<br>4. Observe — moderated (in person or remote) or unmoderated with recordings; ask users to think aloud; don’t help them.<br>5. Measure task success rate, time on task, errors, clicks and satisfaction (e.g. the SUS questionnaire).<br>6. Report issues by severity, with clips or quotes and recommendations.<br><br><b>Related techniques:</b> heuristic evaluation by experts (such as Nielsen’s ten heuristics), A/B tests on live traffic, first-click tests, surveys.<br><br><b>The tester’s role:</b> even without formal studies, testers flag usability problems — unclear labels, confusing errors, too many steps, inconsistent buttons — as observations or improvement tickets that explain the impact on users.',
    followups: ['How is usability testing different from user acceptance testing?', 'How would you report a usability issue that isn’t technically a bug?'],
    mistakes: ['Treating usability as the tester’s personal opinion of the design.', 'Helping users during the session, which hides the real problems.'],
    lesson: 'wm-usability-l10n'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-nfr-10', cat: 'nonfunctional', level: 'beginner', freq: 'common',
    q: 'What is compatibility testing, and how do you plan it?',
    short: 'Compatibility testing checks that the product works across the browsers, operating systems, devices, screen sizes and integrations its users have. Plan it from usage analytics and build a prioritised matrix rather than trying every combination.',
    answer: '<b>What it covers:</b><br>• <b>Browsers</b> — Chrome, Safari, Firefox, Edge and their versions (Safari on iOS is a frequent troublemaker).<br>• <b>Operating systems</b> — Windows, macOS, Android and iOS versions.<br>• <b>Devices and screens</b> — desktop, tablet, phone, high-DPI displays, zoom levels.<br>• <b>Other software and hardware</b> — printers, PDF readers, databases or app servers for on-premises products.<br>• <b>Backward compatibility</b> (old app versions with a new API, files created by older versions) and <b>forward compatibility</b>.<br><br><b>Planning:</b><br>1. Pull analytics: which browsers, OS versions and devices do users really have?<br>2. Build a <b>tiered matrix</b> — full regression on the top combinations, smoke tests on the rest; pairwise techniques cut the number of combinations.<br>3. Mix tools: Playwright across Chromium, Firefox and WebKit, cloud browser and device labs, a few real devices.<br>4. Focus on high-risk areas: layout and CSS, date inputs, file upload, payments, video, fonts.<br><br><b>Typical bugs:</b> layouts breaking in Safari, features using browser APIs that older versions lack, font rendering differences, touch vs mouse events.',
    followups: ['How do you decide which browsers to support?', 'What is the difference between backward and forward compatibility?'],
    mistakes: ['Testing only in Chrome on your own laptop.', 'Trying to test every possible combination instead of prioritising.'],
    lesson: 'wm-cross-browser'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-nfr-11', cat: 'nonfunctional', level: 'beginner', freq: 'common',
    q: 'What is the difference between internationalization (i18n) and localization (L10n) testing?',
    short: 'Internationalization is building the product so it can support any language and region — Unicode, no hard-coded text, flexible layouts. Localization adapts it to one locale — translations, date, number and currency formats, right-to-left layout, cultural fit.',
    answer: '<b>Internationalization (i18n)</b> is an engineering property, done once: text kept in resource files rather than hard-coded, Unicode (UTF-8) end to end, layouts that stretch for longer text, support for right-to-left scripts, locale-aware dates, numbers and currencies, time-zone handling.<br><br><b>i18n testing</b> checks that foundation: enter Urdu, Arabic, Hindi or Chinese text and confirm it is stored, searched, sorted and displayed correctly; use <b>pseudo-localization</b> (text like “[!!! Ŝéţţîñĝš !!!]”) to expose hard-coded strings and truncation before real translations exist.<br><br><b>Localization (L10n)</b> adapts the product for one locale: translated text, date formats (DD/MM/YYYY vs MM/DD/YYYY), number grouping (1,00,000 in India vs 100,000 elsewhere), currency symbol and position, address and phone formats, culturally suitable images and colours, local legal text.<br><br><b>L10n testing</b> checks translations in context (meaning, not just presence), truncated or overlapping labels, untranslated strings, mirrored layout and icons for Urdu or Arabic, correct sorting, and switching locales.<br><br>In short: i18n makes localization possible; L10n does it for each market.',
    followups: ['What is pseudo-localization?', 'What would you check in a right-to-left layout?'],
    mistakes: ['Treating localization as nothing more than translating text.', 'Mixing up which one is engineering preparation and which one is market adaptation.'],
    lesson: 'wm-usability-l10n'
  },
  {
    id: 'iq-nfr-12', cat: 'nonfunctional', level: 'intermediate', freq: 'common',
    q: 'How would you test for broken access control, such as IDOR?',
    short: 'Use several test accounts with different roles. Try to view, edit or delete another user’s data by changing ids in URLs, API paths and bodies, and call admin-only endpoints as a normal user — expecting 403 or 404, never someone else’s data.',
    answer: 'Broken access control tops the OWASP Top 10, and much of it is testable by QA.<br><br><b>IDOR</b> (Insecure Direct Object Reference) is a common form: <code>GET /api/invoices/1045</code> returns my invoice — what happens if I change it to 1046?<br><br><b>Setup:</b> in a test environment, create accounts with different roles and tenants — customer A, customer B, a support agent, an admin.<br><br><b>Tests:</b><br>• <b>Horizontal:</b> as A, request B’s resources by changing ids in the URL, query string, body or hidden fields → expect 403 or 404 with no data in the body.<br>• <b>Vertical:</b> as a normal user, call admin endpoints directly (<code>DELETE /api/users/7</code>) even though the button is hidden in the UI → expect 403.<br>• <b>Writes:</b> PUT, PATCH or DELETE on someone else’s record; changing <code>role</code> or <code>ownerId</code> in a request body.<br>• <b>After logout or a role change:</b> old tokens and sessions no longer grant access.<br>• <b>Lists and search:</b> filters can’t be widened to show other tenants’ data.<br><br>Automate a role × endpoint matrix as API tests — and only test systems you are authorized to test.',
    followups: ['Why isn’t hiding a button in the UI enough?', 'How would you automate access-control checks for many endpoints?'],
    mistakes: ['Testing permissions only through the UI.', 'Accepting a 200 response that contains another user’s data because the screen looked fine.'],
    lesson: 'qb-security'
  }
]);
